unny lines is exactly \\(k_{\\min}\\).\n\n6. **Achieving all intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the construction of Lemma\u202f3, we may replace any non\u2011sunny line by a sunny line of a new slope (different from the three forbidden ones) while keeping the line distinct from the others.  \n   Because a sunny line can be placed to pass through any single uncovered point, each replacement increases \\(k\\) by one without breaking the covering condition.  \n   Repeating this operation we can obtain configurations with any \\(k\\) in the interval \\([k_{\\min},n]\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the integer interval described above.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not in \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) \u2003(\\(n-2\\) lines);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* the anti\u2011diagonal line \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nAll three families have slopes \\( \\in\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), hence are non\u2011sunny.  \nWe claim that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-2\\)*, then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n*If \\(a=n-1\\)*, then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\((a,b)=(n-1,1)\\) lies on the line \\(y=1\\).  \n*If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(b=1\\) and the point \\((n,1)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n\\).  \n\nHence the covering condition holds, and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (Lower bound for the number of sunny lines)\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nEach non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\):\n\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the set in the points \\((c,1),\\dots ,(c,n+1-c)\\), at most \\(n\\) of them;\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) is analogous;\n* a line \\(x+y=c\\) meets the set in the points \\((1,c-1),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), again at most \\(n\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of points that can be covered by the \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(sn\\).  \nSince all \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, we must have  \n\n\\[\nsn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad s\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the total number of lines is \\(n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\le n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nEquivalently  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f3 (Construction attaining the lower bound)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nk_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nWe construct a configuration with exactly \\(k_{\\min}\\) sunny lines.\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k_{\\min}\\) take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\n   \\]\n\n   which has slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny) and passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\).  \n   The lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n2. **Remaining points.**  \n   Remove from \\(S_n\\) all points already covered by the \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n   The number of uncovered points is  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n| - k_{\\min}\\cdot n\n   \\le \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} - \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil n\n   = n - k_{\\min}.\n   \\]\n\n   (The inequality follows from a direct computation using (1).)\n\n3. **Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Because at most \\(n-k_{\\min}\\) points remain uncovered, we can cover