   all have slope \\(-1\\) (therefore are non\u2011sunny) and are distinct.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \n   Thus the family \\(\\{\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of **sunny** lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  How many points of \\(T_n\\) can a single line contain?  \n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n\n* Horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\)).  \n  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b=c\\) gives \\(a\\le n+1-c\\).  \n  Hence the line contains at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n* Vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous and also contains at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n* Diagonal line of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \n  The points of \\(T_n\\) on this line are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and also \\(c-a\\ge1\\).  \n  Thus the number of such points equals \\(c-1\\le n\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\).  \n\nHence **any non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).**  \n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nSuppose two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in T_n\\) lie on it.  \nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=m\\neq -1 .\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1\\le (n+1-a_2)-(a_1+1)=n-a_1-a_2 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1\\ge -(n-a_1-a_2).\n\\]\n\nDividing by the positive integer \\(|a_2-a_1|\\ge1\\) shows that \\(|m|\\le 1\\).  \nSince \\(m\\neq -1,0\\), the only possibilities are \\(m=1\\) or \\(-\\frac12,\\dots\\).  \nA direct check of the integer solutions of \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and the inequalities \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) shows that **at most two points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line**.  \n(Indeed, fixing a slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the equation \\(b=ma+b_0\\) together with the integer constraints forces the \\(x\\)-coordinates of admissible points to differ by at most one, so there cannot be three distinct lattice points.)\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }2\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Counting the points covered by a family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nDenote by \\(s\\) the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the sunny lines, and by \\(t\\) the total number of points that lie on the non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBecause a point may belong to several lines, we only need the **upper bounds** for these totals:\n\n\\[\ns\\le 2k \\qquad\\text{(by (1))},\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt\\le n\\,(n-k) \\qquad\\text{(each of the }n-k\\text{ non\u2011sunny lines contributes at most }n\\text{ points)}.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by at least one line, hence\n\n\\[\ns+t\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert the bounds (2) and (3) into (4):\n\n\\[\n2k+n\\,(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge k\\,(n-2).\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the factor \\(n-2\\) is positive, and (5) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}<1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k\\) must be **zero**:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines\n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nAll have slope \\(-1\\); therefore none is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  \nThe lines are distinct because they have different constant terms.  \nHence this family of \\(n\\) lines satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe counting argument shows that a sunny line can contribute at most two points of the required set, while a non\u2011sunny line can contribute at most \\(n\\) points.  \nWith only \\(n\\) lines available, the inequality (5) forces the number of sunny lines to be zero.  \nThe explicit family of \\(n\\) diagonal lines demonstrates that \\(k=0\\) is indeed attainable.\n\nTherefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]"

