rom \\(a_i+b_i\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1\\le (n+1-a_2)-(a_1+1)=n-a_1-a_2 .\n\\]\n\nSimilarly  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1\\ge -(n-a_1-a_2).\n\\]\n\nDividing by the positive integer \\(|a_2-a_1|\\ge1\\) shows that \\(|m|\\le 1\\).  \nSince \\(m\\neq -1,0\\), the only possibilities are \\(m=1\\) or \\(-\\frac12,\\dots\\).  \nA direct check of the integer solutions of \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and the inequalities \\(a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) shows that **at most two points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line**.  \n(Indeed, fixing a slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), the equation \\(b=ma+b_0\\) together with the integer constraints forces the \\(x\\)-coordinates of admissible points to differ by at most one, so there cannot be three distinct lattice points.)\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }2\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Counting the points covered by a family of \\(n\\) lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nAssume that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \n\nDenote by \\(s\\) the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on the sunny lines, and by \\(t\\) the total number of points that lie on the non‑sunny lines.  \nBecause a point may belong to several lines, we only need the **upper bounds** for these totals:\n\n\\[\ns\\le 2k \\qquad\\text{(by (1))},\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nt\\le n\\,(n-k) \\qquad\\text{(each of the }n-k\\text{ non‑sunny lines contributes at most }n\\text{ points)}.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by at least one line, hence\n\n\\[\ns+t\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nInsert the bounds (2) and (3) into (4):\n\n\\[\n2k+n\\,(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge k\\,(n-2).\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), the factor \\(n-2\\) is positive, and (5) yields\n\n\\[\nk\\le