ts a sunny line can contain.\n\n---\n\n### 2. “Consequently every sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|m|+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n*Analysis:*  \nThe derivation of the bound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|m|+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) relied on the previous case analysis, especially the claim that slopes in \\((-1,0)\\) contribute no points. Since that claim is false, the bound is not universally valid. Moreover, the bound itself is not tight: for slopes with \\(|m|\\) very small (e.g., \\(m=0.1\\)), the expression \\(\\frac{n+1}{|m|+1}\\) can be significantly larger than \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\). Therefore the statement “any sunny line meets at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\)” is **incorrect**.\n\n**Quoted text (critical error #2):**  \n> “The function \\(\\frac{1}{|m|+1}\\) is maximised when \\(|m|=1\\); the corresponding lines have slope \\(1\\) or \\(-2\\). For these slopes the bound becomes  \n> \\[\n> M_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\]”\n\n*Analysis:*  \nThe function \\(f(|m|)=\\frac{1}{|m|+1}\\) is strictly decreasing for \\(|m|>0\\). Hence it attains its maximum as \\(|m|\\to0^+\\), not at \\(|m|=1\\). Consequently the claimed maximal bound \\(M_n\\) is **wrong**. This error propagates to every later inequality that uses \\(M_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3. “Upper bounds for a family of \\(n\\) lines”\n\n**Quoted text:**  \n> “Each non‑sunny line … can intersect \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points … Hence …  \n> \\[\n> t\\le n\\,(n-k),\\qquad s\\le k\\,M_n . \\tag{4}\n> \\]”\n\n*Analysis:*  \nThe bound \\(t\\le n\\,(n-k)\\) for non‑sunny lines is correct (each such line can contain at most \\(n\\) points). However the bound \\(s\\le k\\,M_n\\) depends on the previously established (and false) value of \\(M_n\\). Since \\(M_n\\) is not a valid universal upper bound for a sunny line, inequality (4) is **invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. “Counting condition” and subsequent algebra\n\n