) covers exactly the set \\(T'\\) and contains the same number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines (adding the constant \\(1\\) does not change the slope).\n\n   - **Assemble the whole family.**  \n     Define  \n\n     \\[\n     \\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\{\\ell_V,\\;\\ell_D\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell^{+}\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     This family consists of \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) distinct lines.  \n     It covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the two fixed lines cover the points removed earlier, the lifted family covers the rest).  \n     The number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) is  \n\n     \\[\n     \\underbrace{0}_{\\ell_V\\text{ non\u2011sunny}}+\n     \\underbrace{0}_{\\ell_D\\text{ non\u2011sunny}}+\n     k'=\n     \\begin{cases}\n       k      &\\text{if }k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\n       k-1+1 =k &\\text{if }k=n-1 .\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n\n     Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   The induction is complete, proving that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable family exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the inductive construction shows that this bound is also sufficient.  Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (at least two non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) at least two lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n),\\qquad P_3=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich all belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nIf a line is sunny, its slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line cannot contain two of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) (the slopes of the three possible pairs are exactly \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).  \nA single non\u2011sunny line can contain at most two of them (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains \\(P_1,P_2\\); the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(P_1,P_3\\); the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains \\(P_2,P_3\\)).  \nHence covering the three points requires at least two non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we obtain the necessary inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive construction\n\nWe prove the following statement by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{3,0}= \\{x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=3\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(T_3\\) and contains no sunny line.  \n\nReplacing the diagonal line \\(x+y=3\\) by the sunny line \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\) yields a family with one sunny line, so \\(\\mathcal L_{3,1}\\) exists.  \nSince \\(n-2=1\\), the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2=n-1\\).  \nWe construct \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n1. **Two fixed non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_V:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_D:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n   \\]\n   Both are non\u2011sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\) and all points whose coordinates sum to \\(n+2\\).\n\n2. **Reduced set.**  \n   Remove from \\(T_{n+1}\\) the points already covered by \\(\\ell_V\\) or \\(\\ell_D\\).  The remaining set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) is a bijection \\(T'\\to T_n\\).\n\n3. **Choice of the parameter for the induction hypothesis.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k      &\\text{if }k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\n   k-1    &\\text{if }k=n-1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases \\(0\\le k'\\le (n)-2\\).\n\n4. **Apply the induction hypothesis to \\(T_n\\).**  \n   There exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Lift the family to the original coordinates.**  \n   For each line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\) with equation \\(Ax+By=C\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell^{+}:\\;A(x-1)+B(y-1)=C .\n   \\]\n\n   The collection \\(\\mathcal F^{+}=\\{\\ell^{+}\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers precisely the set \\(T'\\), and contains the same number \\(k'\\) of sunny lines (the slope is unchanged).\n\n6. **Assemble the final family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\{\\ell_V,\\;\\ell_D\\}\\cup\\mathcal F^{+}.\n   \\]\n\n   This family has \\(2+n=n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and contains  \n\n   \\[\n   0+0+k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k      &\\text{if }k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\n   (k-1)+1 =k &\\text{if }k=n-1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) satisfies the required properties.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n+1\\), completing the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof\n\nLemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe inductive construction shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a family exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"

