            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (at least two non‑sunny lines)\n\nIn any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) at least two lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n),\\qquad P_3=(n,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe slopes of the three possible pairs are \\(0\\) (between \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_3\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (between \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\)) and \\(-1\\) (between \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\)).  \nA sunny line cannot contain two of the points, because its slope would have to be one of the forbidden values.  \nA single non‑sunny line can contain at most two of them (the vertical line \\(x=1\\) contains \\(P_1,P_2\\); the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains \\(P_1,P_3\\); the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) contains \\(P_2,P_3\\)).  \nThus covering the three points requires at least two non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\nConsequently, if a family of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) contains \\(k\\) sunny lines, then  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) the following statement:\n\n> **Claim.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family, and  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{3,0}= \\{x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all six points; all three lines have slopes \\( \\infty,0,-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \n\nReplacing the diagonal line \\(x+y=4\\) by the sunny line \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\) gives a family with one sunny line, i.e. \\(\\mathcal L_{3,1}\\).  \nSince \\(n-2=1\\), the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the claim holds for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le (n+1)-2=n-1\\).  We construct \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n1. **Two fixed non‑sunny lines.**  \n   \\[\n   \\ell_V:\\;x=1,\\qquad \\ell_D:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n   \\]\n   Both are non‑sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\) and all points whose coordinates sum to \\(n+2\\); in particular they contain the three extreme points \\((1,1),(1,n+1),(n+1,1)\\).\n\n2. **Reduced point set.**  \n   Remove from \\(T_{n+1}\\) the points already covered by \\(\\ell_V\\) or \\(\\ell_D\\).  The remaining set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T'=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) is a bijection  \n\n   \\[\n   T'\\;\\longleftrightarrow\\;T_{\\,n-1}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Parameter for the induction hypothesis.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k      &\\text{if }k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\n   k-1    &\\text{if }k=n-1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), we have \\(0\\le k'\\le (n-1)-2\\); therefore the induction hypothesis can be applied to the set \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) with the number \\(k'\\).\n\n4. **Apply the induction hypothesis.**  \n   There exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Lift the family.**  \n   For each line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\) with equation \\(Ax+By=C\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell^{+}:\\;A(x-1)+B(y-1)=C .\n   \\]\n\n   The collection \\(\\mathcal F^{+}=\\{\\ell^{+}\\mid\\ell\\in\\mathcal F\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines, covers precisely the set \\(T'\\) (by (4)), and contains exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines (translation does not change the slope).\n\n6. **Assemble the whole family.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\{\\ell_V,\\;\\ell_D\\}\\cup\\mathcal F^{+}.\n   \\]\n\n   This family has \\(2+(n-1)=n+1\\) distinct lines, covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\), and the number of sunny lines equals  \n\n   \\[\n   0+0+k'=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   k      &\\text{if }k\\le n-2,\\\\[2pt]\n   (k-1)+1 =k &\\text{if }k=n-1 .\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus the claim holds for \\(n+1\\).  By induction it holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final conclusion\n\nLemma 1 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe inductive construction shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each corr