\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in T\\}\\).\n\nAltogether we have \\(2+k\\) distinct lines.  \nIf \\(2+k=n\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(2+k<n\\) we add further lines as follows.\n\nSelect any \\((n-2-k)\\) points of \\(S_{2}\\setminus T\\) and for each of them take the corresponding sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\); denote these extra lines by \\(\\ell_{a,b}^{\\prime}\\).  \nThese extra lines are still sunny and each of them covers a point of \\(S\\) that was not yet covered, so the covering condition remains satisfied.\n\nIf after this step we still have fewer than \\(n\\) lines (this can happen only when \\(k<n-2\\) and we deliberately omitted some of the \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) to keep the number of sunny lines exactly \\(k\\)), we finally add arbitrary sunny lines that avoid **all** points of \\(S\\) (for instance, the line \\(y=2x+100\\)).  \nSuch lines do not affect the covering property and increase the total number of lines to exactly \\(n\\).\n\nIn the final family we have:\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines that are needed to cover points of \\(S\\);\n* the two fixed non‑sunny lines;\n* possibly additional sunny lines that avoid \\(S\\).\n\nThus we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom part 1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nPart 3 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\] ∎"
