)+(k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nBoth parts together give the complete description of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=c\\)).  \nConsequently a **non‑sunny** line is one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{horizontal:}&\\quad y=c,\\\\\n\\text{vertical:}&\\quad x=c,\\\\\n\\text{diagonal:}&\\quad x+y=c .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound: at least two lines must be non‑sunny  \n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that an admissible configuration contains at most one non‑sunny line.  \nIf there is **no** non‑sunny line, then all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nA sunny line can meet the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, because its slope is not \\(\\infty\\).  \nHence at most one of the points \\((1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n)\\) can lie on a given sunny line.  \nSince there are \\(n\\) such points and only \\(n\\) sunny lines, each of the points \\((1,i)\\) would have to be covered by a distinct sunny line.\n\nNow consider the point \\((n,1)\\).  \nA sunny line that contains \\((1,i)\\) has equation  \n\n\\[\ny-i = m\\,(x-1),\\qquad m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1 .\n\\]\n\nPlugging \\(x=n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = i + m\\,(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq 0\\) the right‑hand side is different for different values of \\(i\\); consequently none of the \\(n\\) sunny lines that pass through the points \\((1,i)\\) can also pass through \\((n,1)\\).  \nThus \\((n,1)\\) would remain uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nIf there is **exactly one** non‑sunny line, the same argument applies to the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, which can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points among \\((1,1),\\dots ,(1,n)\\).  \nAgain \\((n,1)\\) would be uncovered.  \n\nHence any admissible configuration must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines.  \nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering Lemma – covering \\(T_m\\) with \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines  \n\n> **Lemma 1 (Covering Lemma).**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal N_m\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines such that every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(m=2q+r\\) with \\(q=\\bigl\\lfloor m/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) and \\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n*Horizontal lines.* For each integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le q\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{(h)}_b:\\; y=b .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines.* For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le q\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{(v)}_a:\\; x=a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r=1\\) (i.e. \\(m\\) is odd) add the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{(d)}:\\; x+y=m+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N_m=\\{\\ell^{(h)}_b\\mid 1\\le b\\le q\\}\\cup\n            \\{\\ell^{(v)}_a\\mid 1\\le a\\le q\\}\\cup\n            \\begin{cases}\n               \\{\\ell^{(d)}\\}, & r=1,\\\\[2pt]\n               \\varnothing,   & r=0 .\n            \\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N_m|=2q+r=m .\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct because they have different defining constants (different \\(a\\), \\(b\\) or, when present, a different slope).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le q\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell^{(v)}_a\\).  \n* If \\(b\\le q\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell^{(h)}_b\\).  \n* If neither inequality holds, then \\(a>q\\) and \\(b>q\\).  \n  Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  a+b > 2q = m-r .\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\((a,b)\\in T_m\\) we also have \\(a+b\\le m+1\\); the only possibility is \\(a+b=m+1\\) and \\(r=1\\).  \n  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(\\ell^{(d)}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N_m\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }1\\le m\\le n).\n\\]\n\nApply Lemma 1 with this \\(m\\) and obtain a family \\(\\mathcal N_m\\) of \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\nObserve that  \n\n\\[\nT_n = T_m\\;\\cup\\; R,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b>m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m=n-k\\), the inequality \\(a+b>m+1\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge n-k+2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(R\\) consists of the lattice points that lie on the \\(k\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; a+b = n-k+1+j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_j\\) has slope \\(-1\\); however, to obtain **sunny** lines we perturb each of them slightly, giving them distinct slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nBecause the points of a given \\(L_j\\) are collinear, a small rotation about any point of \\(L_j\\) produces a sunny line that still contains **all** points of \\(L_j\\).  \nSince the \\(k\\) values of \\(j\\) are distinct, the resulting sunny lines are distinct.\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) the set of these \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy construction every point of \\(R\\) lies on exactly one line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\), and every point of \\(T_m\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N_m\\).  \nTherefore the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}= \\mathcal N_m\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nThe cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_{n,k}| = |\\mathcal N_m|+|\\mathcal S_k| = m+k = (n-k)+k = n .\n\\]\n\nMoreover, by construction exactly the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) are sunny, while all lines of \\(\\mathcal N_m\\) are non‑sunny.  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) satisfies the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\n* From Section 2 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n* From Sections 3–4 we have exhibited, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines meeting the covering requirement and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
