s \\(D_c\\) by sunny lines that still pass through the same set of points.\n\n   For a fixed \\(c\\) (\\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) the points on \\(D_c\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\).  \n   Choose any two distinct points among them, say \\((p,c-p)\\) and \\((q,c-q)\\) with \\(p\\neq q\\).  \n   The line through these two points has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{(c-p)-(c-q)}{p-q}= -1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. it is exactly \\(D_c\\).  \n   To obtain a *sunny* line we must keep **one** of the points and replace the other by a point outside \\(D_c\\) while preserving coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   This can be done as follows.  \n   For each selected diagonal \\(D_c\\) we keep the point \\((1,c-1)\\) on the line and replace the remaining \\(c-2\\) points by a single sunny line that passes through \\((1,c-1)\\) and an extra point \\((c,1)\\) (which lies in column \\(x=c\\) and row \\(y=1\\)).  \n   The line through \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c,1)\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_c=\\frac{1-(c-1)}{c-1}= -\\frac{c-2}{c-1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is **different** from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(c\\ge 2\\); hence it is sunny.  \n\n   This sunny line contains exactly the two points \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c,1)\\).  \n   The point \\((c,1)\\) is already covered by the horizontal line \\(L_1\\); therefore after the replacement the set of covered points does not change.\n\n   Performing this replacement for any \\(k\\) distinct values of \\(c\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)) yields a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\text{sunny line }S_c\\mid c\\in C\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{\\,D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n,\\;c\\notin C\\,\\bigr\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(C\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(|C|=k\\).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of:\n   * the two fixed non‑sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2\\);\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_c\\);\n   * \\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines \\(D_c\\).\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal