=\\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct **non‑sunny** lines and covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n   **Step 3 – turning selected diagonals into sunny lines without losing coverage.**  \n\n   For a fixed \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) consider the two points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_c:=(1,c-1),\\qquad Q_c:=(c,1).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Both belong to the diagonal \\(D_c\\).  \n   Define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_c:=\\text{the line through }P_c\\text{ and }Q_c .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_c=\\frac{1-(c-1)}{c-1}= -\\frac{c-2}{c-1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) for every \\(c\\ge 2\\); hence \\(S_c\\) is sunny.\n\n   Observe that every point of the diagonal \\(D_c\\) other than \\(P_c\\) and \\(Q_c\\) has the form \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le c-2\\).  \n   Such a point also lies on the diagonal \\(D_{c'}\\) where \\(c'=a+(c-a)=c\\); therefore it is **not** covered elsewhere.  \n   To keep the covering property we do **not** replace the whole diagonal \\(D_c\\) by \\(S_c\\); instead we keep \\(D_c\\) and **add** the sunny line \\(S_c\\) while removing a different diagonal that is already redundant.\n\n   The redundancy comes from the fact that the two fixed non‑sunny lines already cover all points with \\(b=1\\) (by \\(L_1\\)) and all points with \\(a=n-1\\) and \\(b\\le 2\\) (by \\(L_2\\)).  \n   Consequently, for the largest diagonal \\(D_n\\) the points \\((1,n-1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) are already covered by \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) respectively; the remaining points of \\(D_n\\) are \\((2,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\).  \n   These points are also present on the diagonals \\(D_{n-1},D_{n-2},\\dots ,D_2\\) (because, for example, \\((2,n-2)\\) lies on \\(D_{n}\\) and on \\(D_{n-2}\\) as \\(2+(n-2)=n\\)).  \n   Hence **one** diagonal among \\(\\{D_2,\\dots ,D_n\\}\\) can be omitted without losing any covered point: we simply discard \\(D