ause\n   \\[\n   a+(c-a)=c=(c-1)+1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. it is the point \\((a,\\,c-1-a+1)=(a,c-a)\\) of \\(D_{c-1}\\) shifted by one unit along the horizontal direction.  Consequently **all interior points of \\(D_c\\) are already covered by the diagonal \\(D_{c-1}\\)**.  (For \\(c=2\\) there is no interior point, so the statement is vacuous.)\n\n   Because of this redundancy we may **discard the diagonal \\(D_c\\)** and **replace it by the sunny line \\(S_c\\)** without losing any covered point:\n   * the two endpoints of \\(D_c\\) are still covered (they lie on \\(S_c\\));\n   * every other point of \\(D_c\\) is already covered by \\(D_{c-1}\\).\n\n   Applying this replacement independently for any collection of indices\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal I=\\{c_1,c_2,\\dots ,c_k\\}\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   we obtain the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{\\mathcal I}:=\n   \\{H_1,V_{\\,n-1}\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl(\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{D_{c_i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\bigr)\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{S_{c_i}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal F_{\\mathcal I}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_{c_i}\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the two fixed lines together with the remaining diagonals).  By the discussion above every point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered, and all lines are distinct because each \\(S_{c_i}\\) has a slope different from \\(-1\\) while every retained diagonal has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n5. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\)**  \n\n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) choose an arbitrary subset \\(\\mathcal I\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) of size \\(k\\) and set \\(\\mathcal F_k:=\\mathcal F_{\\mathcal I}\\).  \n   Then \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) is a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility for larger \\(k\\)**  \n\n   If \\(k\\ge n-1\\) then the configurat