            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define the two lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y=\\frac{t-1}{t}\\,x+\\frac{1}{t},\n\\qquad\nd_t:\\; x+y=t .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Claim 1.*  \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny and \\(d_t\\) is non‑sunny.  \n\n*Proof.*  The slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(\\frac{t-1}{t}\\).  Since \\(t\\ge2\\), this number is neither \\(0\\) (horizontal), nor undefined (vertical), nor \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence \\(\\ell_t\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, i.e. it is sunny.  The line \\(d_t\\) has slope \\(-1\\), so it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny. ∎\n\n*Claim 2.*  For each \\(t\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t\\cap T_n = d_t\\cap T_n = \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b=t\\).  Substituting \\(b=t-a\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nt-a = \\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nt = a + \\frac{t-1}{t}\\,a + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nt = a + \\frac{t-1}{t}a + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to an identity, confirming that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_t\\).  Conversely, if \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_t\\) with integer \\(a\\ge1\\), solving (4) for \\(b\\) yields \\(b = t-a\\); the conditions \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) force \\(a+b=t\\).  The same reasoning shows that the points of \\(d_t\\) inside the triangle are exactly those with \\(a+b=t\\). ∎\n\nThus each diagonal \\(x+y=t\\) of the integer triangle \\(T_n\\) can be covered either by the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) or by the non‑sunny line \\(d_t\\).\n\nNow fix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Choose a subset  \n\n\\[\nS\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\\qquad |S|=k .\n\\]\n\nConstruct the family of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L =\\{\\ell_t\\mid t\\in S\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{d_t\\mid t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus S\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  For distinct values \\(t\\neq t'\\) the slopes \\(\\frac{t-1}{t}\\) and \\(\\frac{t'-1}{t'}\\) are different, so the lines \\(\\ell_t\\) are pairwise distinct.  All lines \\(d_t\\) have the same slope \\(-1\\) but different intercepts, hence they are also distinct from each other and from any \\(\\ell_{t'}\\).  Consequently \\(|\\mathcal L| = |S| + (n - |S|) = n\\).\n\n*Covering property.*  By (5), for each \\(t\\) the diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=t\\}\\) is contained in the line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that corresponds to \\(t\\) (either \\(\\ell_t\\) or \\(d_t\\)).  Since the diagonals for \\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) partition \\(T_n\\), the union of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) equals \\(T_n\\).  Hence every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of the family.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  Exactly the lines \\(\\ell_t\\) with \\(t\\in S\\) are sunny (Claim 1), and there are \\(|S|=k\\) of them.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the required condition with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nBecause the construction works for **any** \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), all these values are admissible.  No other value can occur, since the total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge