t{Vertical lines: }   x=i\\quad (i=1,\\dots ,k),\\\\\n     &\\text{Sunny lines: }      \\ell_{t}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}\\qquad (t=1,\\dots ,k+2),\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     where each \\(\\ell_{t}\\) passes through the lattice point \\((t,\\,n+1-t)\\) (which lies on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\)) and has slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\neq 0,-1\\).  \n   * One checks directly that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) belongs either to a horizontal line, a vertical line, or to the unique sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) with \\(t=a\\) (if \\(a\\le k+2\\)) or to the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (if \\(b\\le n-2-k\\)). Hence the covering condition holds.\n\n3. Combining the lower bound and the explicit construction shows that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary condition: at least two non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the three “border” subsets of \\(T_{n}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nH &=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nV &=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nD &=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\(|H|=|V|=n\\) and \\(|D|=n-1\\); the three sets intersect only at \\((1,1)\\), so  \n\n\\[\n|H\\cup V\\cup D| = 2n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) can contain at most one point of \\(H\\); a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical) can contain at most one point of \\(V\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain at most one point of \\(D\\).  \nConsequently, if we have \\(h\\) horizontal, \\(v\\) vertical and \\(d\\) slope \\(-1\\) lines among the \\(n\\) covering lines, then the total number of points of \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince all points of \\(H\\cup V\\cup D\\) must be covered, (1) and (2) give  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d\\ge 2n-1.\n\\]\n\nBut each line belongs to at most one of the three families, so  \n\n\\[\nh+v+d\\le n.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(n\\ge 2n-1\\), a contradiction unless at least two of the numbers \\(h,v,d\\) are non‑zero. In other words, **among the \\(n\\) lines at least two are non‑sunny**. Therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Horizontal lines.**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\ny=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2-k). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-2-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n\n**Vertical lines.**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nx=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\nThus far we have used \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines; the remaining two lines must be sunny.\n\n**Sunny lines.**  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k+2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); hence each \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover, substituting \\(x=t\\) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,t + \\frac{1}{t+1}= \\frac{t^{2}+1}{t+1}= n+1-t,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\ell_{t}\\) passes through the lattice point \\((t,\\,n+1-t)\\in D\\).  \nThe \\(k+2\\) lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k+2}\\) are distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\nNow we have exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-2-k)+k+(k+2)=n\n\\]\n\nlines, of which the \\(k+2\\) lines \\(\\ell_{t}\\) are sunny and the remaining \\(n-2\\) are non‑sunny.  \nHence the number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+2-(n-2)=k. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Verification that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered.**  \n\n*If a point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b\\le n-2-k\\),* it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) from (4).  \n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\),* it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (5).  \n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and \\(b\\ge n-1-k\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only possibility is \\(a+b=n+1\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in D\\).  \nWrite \\(a=t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k+2\\) (the inequality follows from \\(a\\ge k+1\\) and \\(a\\le n\\)).  \nThen \\((a,b)=(t,\\,n+1-t)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) defined in (6).  \n\nHence every lattice point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe necessary condition (3) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction above shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]"
