            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand no other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) works.\n\n**b. Method Sketch** –  \n\n1. **Necessary condition.**  \n   *Consider the three “border’’ sets of lattice points*  \n\n   \\[\n   H=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad \n   V=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad \n   D=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\ a,b\\ge 1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   They contain \\(2n-1\\) distinct points.  \n   A line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis can meet at most one point of \\(H\\); a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis can meet at most one point of \\(V\\); a line of slope \\(-1\\) can meet at most one point of \\(D\\).  \n   Hence covering all \\(2n-1\\) points forces at least two of the \\(n\\) covering lines to be non‑sunny, i.e. parallel to one of the three forbidden directions. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sufficiency – explicit construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{Horizontal lines: } y=j\\quad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1),\\\\\n   &\\text{One vertical line: }   x=1 .\n   \\end{