r \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Write \\(k=2m\\) or \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is even* (\\(k=2m\\)):  \n   – Choose any \\(m\\) distinct diagonals, say those with indices \\(t_1,\\dots ,t_m\\).  \n   – For each chosen diagonal replace the diagonal line \\(x+y=t_i\\) by the two sunny lines \\(S_{t_i}^{(1)},S_{t_i}^{(2)}\\) constructed in (2).  \n   – For the remaining \\(n-2m\\) diagonals keep the original non‑sunny diagonal lines.  \n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   2m\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-2m)\\;(\\text{non‑sunny}) = n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(2m=k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd* (\\k=2m+1\\)):  \n   – Perform the even construction for \\(2m\\) sunny lines as above.  \n   – For one additional diagonal (choose any not already altered) keep the diagonal line **and** add a single extra sunny line that passes through a single point of that diagonal (for instance the line through \\((1,t-1)\\) with slope \\(2\\)).  \n   – This adds one more sunny line without increasing the total number of lines beyond \\(n\\) because we replace a non‑sunny diagonal by **the same diagonal plus an extra sunny line**, and then we delete one of the already‑present non‑sunny diagonals (which is permissible because its points are already covered by the newly added sunny line). A concrete implementation is:  \n\n     * keep the diagonal \\(x+y=t_0\\) for some \\(t_0\\);  \n     * add the sunny line \\(y=2x+(t_0-2)\\) (which passes through \\((1,t_0-1)\\) and is sunny);  \n     * delete the diagonal \\(x+y=t_1\\) for a different index \\(t_1\\); the points of that deleted diagonal are already covered by the two sunny lines used for the \\(2m\\) diagonals.  \n\n   The resulting family still contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and now has \\(2m+1=k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   *Each diagonal that is kept unchanged* is covered by its diagonal line.  \n   *Each diagonal that is replaced by two sunny lines* is covered because, by construction (2), the union of those two lines contains every point of the diagonal.  \n   *The extra sunny line used in the odd‑\\(k\\) case* covers the single point \\((1,t_0-1)\\); this point is already on the diagonal \\(x+y=t_0\\), so coverage is unchanged.  \n   Hence **every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the final family**.\n\n6. **Distinctness of the lines.**  \n   All diagonal lines have distinct intercepts, so they are different.  \n   For each diagonal the two sunny lines \\(S_t^{(1)},S_t^{(2)}\\) have distinct slopes (different \\(p,q\\)), and their slopes are never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently no sunny line coincides with any diagonal line or with any other sunny line belonging to a different diagonal.  \n   The extra sunny line used for odd \\(k\\) has slope \\(2\\), which does not appear among the slopes of the other sunny lines (they are of the form \\(\\frac{t-p-1}{p-1}\\) with \\(p\\ge2\\)), so it is also distinct.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions, and clearly no other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For any }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
