e line \\(x+y=t\\). … For a diagonal \\(D_t\\) with \\(t\\in R\\) we must cover its \\(|D_t|=t-1\\) points with the other lines. By Lemma 1 each line can contribute **at most one** point of \\(D_t\\). Moreover, a line that is itself a diagonal (i.e. belongs to \\(S\\)) is parallel to \\(D_t\\) and therefore contributes **zero** points of \\(D_t\\). Consequently only the \\(k=n-s\\) sunny lines can be used for the diagonals in \\(R\\).”  \n  **Verification:** The reasoning up to this point is sound. The conclusion that only sunny lines can contribute to the diagonals in \\(R\\) follows from the fact that diagonal lines in \\(S\\) are parallel to those in \\(R\\).\n\n- **Quote (1):** “\\(P_R=\\sum_{t\\in R}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\sum_{t\\in S}(t-1).\\)”  \n  **Verification:** Correct; total number of points in \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n- **Quote (2):** “Each sunny line meets every diagonal in \\(R\\) in at most one point, so a single sunny line can cover at most \\(|R|\\) points of \\(P_R\\). With \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain the capacity bound \\(k\\cdot |R|\\ge P_R\\).”  \n  **Verification:** The bound follows from Lemma 1 applied to each diagonal in \\(R\\). The statement is correct, but the proof does not explicitly restate Lemma 1 here; the connection is implicit (minor justification gap).\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Critical step – substitution \\(|R| = n-s\\)**  \n\n- **Quote:** “Since \\(|R|=n-s\\) and \\(k=n-s\\), inequality (2) becomes \\((n-s)^2\\ge \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\sum_{t\\in S}(t-1).\\)”  \n\n- **Issue:** The equality \\(|R|=n-s\\) is **not justified**. By definition \\(|R| = n - |S|\\) because there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonal indices \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). The number \\(s\\) counts **all** non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, and diagonal). Some of those non‑sunny lines may be horizontal or vertical, which do **not** belong to \\(S\\). Consequently \\(|S|\\le s\\) and therefore \\(|R| = n-|S| \\ge n-s\\). Replac