at worst case (3.3) becomes  \n\n   \\[\n   n(n-s)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2},\n   \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\n   (n-s)^2\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{3.4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3.4) for \\(s\\) gives the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\ge\\;n-\\sqrt{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}} .\n   \\tag{3.5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(s\\) is an integer we may write the sharp bound  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil n-\\sqrt{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3.6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-s\\;\\le\\; \\Bigl\\lfloor\\sqrt{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3.7}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (3.7) is **necessary**.\n\n4. **Sufficiency – construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le K:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\sqrt{\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\qquad s=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   We shall build a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – choose the non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Write \\(s=h+v\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   h=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{s}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad v=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{s}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Take the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=h\n   \\]\n\n   and the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(s\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \n   They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a\\le v\\) or \\(b\\le h\\).  \n   The **remaining** points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>v,\\;b>h\\}.\n   \\tag{4.1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-h-1=n-h .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   v<a\\le n-h .\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the rectangle containing \\(U\\) has dimensions  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-h)-v\\;\\times\\;(n-v)-h .\n   \\]\n\n   A direct computation using \\(h+v=s=n-k\\) shows that the number