ed above.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \nIts cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{y}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal, or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nare distinct and non‑sunny.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence the union of the \\(n\\) lines (3) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore a configuration with **zero** sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Impossibility of a configuration with \\(k=n\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(c(L)=|L\\cap T_n|\\) the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).  \nSumming over all lines we have  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}c(L)\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsider a vertical line \\(x=i\\) (as in Lemma 1).  \nFrom (4) we have  \n\n\\[\nc(x=i)=n+1-i\\le n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNow let \\(L\\) be any sunny line.  \nBecause \\(L\\) is not parallel to the three directions in (2), its intersection with the integer lattice is a set of points whose coordinates satisfy a non‑trivial linear relation with non‑zero coefficients for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).  \nConsequently, when we restrict to the triangular region \\(T_n\\), the number of lattice points on \\(L\\) cannot exceed the maximum number that a vertical line can have, i.e.\n\n\\[\nc(L)\\le n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, equality in (7) can occur only when \\(L\\) coincides with a vertical line, which is impossible because \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nHence for every sunny line \\(L\\),\n\n\\[\nc(L)\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8) for all \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}c(L)\\le n\\,(n-1)=n^{2}-n .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n < \\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nthe right‑hand side being the value from (1).  \nThus (9) contradicts the necessary inequality (5).  \nTherefore a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines is impossible; i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Consequences\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have the admissible value \\(k=0\\).  \nFrom Lemma 2 we obtain the restriction \\(k\\neq n\\).  \nNo further restrictions have been proved; the problem of determining whether the intermediate values \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) are all attainable remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer (partial)**  \n\nThe set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is a non‑empty proper subset of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIn particular  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\text{ is always possible, while }n\\text{ is never possible}.}\n\\]\n\nThe status of the intermediate values \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) is not settled by the arguments presented above."
