r cover exactly the remaining points \\(R\\), while the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\) cover the rest of \\(T_n\\). The total family consists of \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n   This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur; Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) does occur. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_y:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=n\\) is impossible\n\nAssume that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\).\nFor a line \\(L\\) write  \n\n\\[\nc(L)=|L\\cap T_n|.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, each point of \\(T_n\\) is counted at most once, so  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}c(L)=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any direction in (2); consequently its intersection\nwith the integer lattice is a set of points whose coordinates satisfy a non\u2011trivial linear relation with non\u2011zero coefficients for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Inside the triangular region \\(T_n\\) such a line can contain at most \\(n\\) lattice points, and it can contain exactly \\(n\\) points only when it coincides with a vertical line (the only direction that can achieve the maximal count). Since all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny, we have  \n\n\\[\nc(L)\\le n-1\\qquad\\text{for every }L\\in\\mathcal L .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSumming (5) over the \\(n\\) lines gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal L}c(L)\\le n(n-1)=n^{2}-n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (4). Hence a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist; i.e. \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(V_i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nso the union of the lines (8) equals \\(T_n\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for any \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f1 \u2013 vertical part.**  \nUse the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n**Step\u202f2 \u2013 the remaining points.**  \nThe set of points still uncovered is  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>m\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=m+1+t\\) with \\(t\\ge0\\) and using the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nR=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\Bigl\\{\\,\\bigl(m+1+t(k+1),\\;n+2-j+t\\,j\\bigr)\\;\\Big|\\;\nt=0,1,\\dots ,\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-m-1}{k+1}\\Big\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(R\\) splits naturally into \\(k\\) disjoint arithmetic progressions, one for each \\(j\\).\n\n**Step\u202f3 \u2013 sunny lines covering \\(R\\).**  \nFor each