(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Start from \\(\\mathcal F_{n-2}\\) and replace \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(S_i\\) by any non‑sunny lines that already pass through points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered (for instance additional horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) or vertical lines \\(x=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\)).  \n   Because each such replacement still contains at least the point \\((1,1)\\) (which is already covered by \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\)), the covering property is preserved.  \n   After exactly \\(n-(k+2)=n-2-k\\) replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely the original \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \n\n   Hence for every admissible \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit constructions yields the complete answer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All admissible values are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
