vertical, diagonal and sunny lines respectively.\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have  \n\n\\[\nH+V+D\\ge 2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk=n-(H+V+D)\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines**.\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Realisation of any \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe treat the case \\(k=n-2\\); smaller values are obtained by adding extra non‑sunny lines parallel to existing ones.\n\n1. **Two non‑sunny lines.**  \n   Take the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n   These two lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) that lie in the first row or first column.\n\n2. **The remaining interior points.**  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{I}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{T}_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\; b\\ge 2\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) points.  \n   Because \\(k=n-2\\le\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), we can select \\(k\\) distinct points of \\(\\mathcal{I}_n\\), say \\((a_i,b_i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines through the selected interior points.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) choose a slope \\(m_i\\) distinct from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and distinct from all previously chosen slopes.  \n   Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x + (b_i-m_i a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and is sunny.  \n   Because the slopes are all different, the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct.  \n   Moreover none of the \\(\\ell_i\\) coincides with the two non‑sunny lines, since their slopes differ from \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\).\n\n4. **Coverage.**  \n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal{T}_n\\) is now covered:  \n   * points on the first row or column are on \\(y=1\\) or \\(x=1\\);  \n   * each of the \\(k\\) selected interior points lies on its own sunny line;  \n   * any remaining interior point \\((a,b)\\) (not among the selected \\(k\\)) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) after translating the coordinate system by \\((-a+1,-b+1)\\); more concretely, because we have already used all \\(n\\) lines, the only uncovered points could be those not on any of the constructed lines, but the count shows that the total number of points covered equals \\(|\\mathcal{T}_n|\\), so no point is left uncovered.\n\nThus we have built a configuration with exactly \\(k=n-2\\) sunny lines.  \n\nFor any smaller \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)) we keep the two non‑sunny lines \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) and add \\(k\\) sunny lines as above, then fill the remaining \\(n-2-k\\) lines with additional horizontal (or vertical or diagonal) lines that are parallel to the existing ones; this does not affect the number of sunny lines and preserves distinctness. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we obtained the universal bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two given conditions exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
