            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{\\text{horizontal lines in the configuration}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\text{vertical lines}\\},\\;\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\text{lines parallel to }x+y=0\\},\n\\]\n\nand let  \n\n\\[\nH=|\\mathcal H|,\\;V=|\\mathcal V|,\\;D=|\\mathcal D|,\\;\nk=n-(H+V+D)\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of horizontal, vertical, diagonal and sunny lines, respectively.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n,1),\\qquad P_3=(1,n),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  \nA sunny line has slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it can contain **at most one** of the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  \nSince each of these points must lie on some line of the configuration, at least two of them are covered by non‑sunny lines, i.e. by a line belonging to \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\).  \nHence at least two of the three families are non‑empty and\n\n\\[\nH+V+D\\ge 2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the total number of lines equals \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nk=n-(H+V+D)\\le n-2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(k\\le n-2\\) for every admissible configuration**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(a) Non‑sunny lines.**  \nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_v:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_h^{(j)}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(1+(n-k-1)=n-k\\) distinct lines, all non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(b) Sunny lines.**  \nDefine the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\mid b\\ge n-k\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal I_n\\) then  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(a\\ge2\\) there is exactly one admissible \\(b\\), namely \\(b=n+1-a\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I_n=\\{(1,n),\\;(2,n-1),\\;(3,n-2),\\dots ,(k+1,n-k)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,n)\\) already lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\); the remaining \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i+1,\\;n-i)\\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1)\n\\]\n\nare distinct.\n\nChoose \\(k\\) pairwise different real numbers  \n\n\\[\nm_0,m_1,\\dots ,m_{k-1}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=m_i x +\\bigl(n-i-m_i(i+1)\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEquation (4) is obtained by forcing \\(\\ell_i\\) to pass through \\(P_i\\).  \nBecause the slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct, the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct and none of them is horizontal, vertical or diagonal; therefore they are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(c) Verification of the covering property.**  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\)*, then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (one of the \\(L_h^{(j)}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\)*, then by the description (3) we have \\((a,b)=P_{a-2}\\), and consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a-2}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  \nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(d) Smaller values of \\(k\\).**  \nWhen \\(k<n-2\\) the above construction already yields a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nIf one wishes to increase the number of non‑sunny lines while keeping the same \\(k\\), one may replace some of the horizontal lines \\(y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k-1\\) by additional vertical or diagonal lines parallel to the existing ones; this does not affect the covering property nor the count of sunny lines.\n\nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final answer  \n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) (proved in §1) and the sufficient construction for all \\(k\\le n-2\\) (proved in §2) together give the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
