            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have repaired the gaps pointed out in the bug report and now present a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   H,\\;V,\\;D\\in\\mathbb N\n   \\]\n\n   be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) lines that appear in a configuration and put  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (the number of sunny lines).\n\n2. **Necessary upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The three lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(1,n)\n   \\]\n\n   belong to the required set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them.  \n   Consequently at least two of the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) must be non‑empty, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   H+V+D\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(k\\le n-2\\) is a necessary condition.\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:\\;x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}^{(j)}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(1+(n-k-1)=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-2t).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{t}\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   We shall prove that the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{0},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) together with the lines introduced above cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   ----  \n\n   **Lemma 1.** *Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) with \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_{0},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   For such a point we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(b\\ge n-k\\),\n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting \\(a\\) from (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\le b-a\\le n+1-2a .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\le n-k\\) (otherwise \\(b\\ge n-k\\) would contradict (5)), the right–hand side of (7) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-2a\\ge n+1-2(n-k)=n-2k+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The left–hand side of (7) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\le n-k-2 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (8) and (9) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2k\\le b-a\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The interval \\([\\,n-2k,\\;n-2\\,]\\) contains exactly the \\(k\\) even integers  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2k,\\;n-2(k-1),\\dots ,n-2 .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(n\\) is odd the interval contains the same odd integers; in either case the parity of all numbers in (10) equals the parity of \\(n\\).)  \n   Hence there exists a unique integer \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   b-a = n-2t .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (12) is precisely the equation of the line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) given in (4).  \n   Therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{t}\\), and the uniqueness of \\(t\\) shows that it lies on **exactly one** of the sunny lines. ∎\n\n   ----  \n\n   **Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) is covered.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   * If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on one of the lines \\(L_{h}^{(j)}\\).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), i.e. on \\(L_{v}\\).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) then Lemma 1 applies and \\((a,b)\\) lies on a unique sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\).\n\n   Thus every lattice point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   Because the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for all such \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above.