"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have examined the bug report and agree that the previous Lemma\u202f1 was not proved correctly.  \nBelow I give a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *non\u2011sunny* iff it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   H,\\;V,\\;D\\in\\mathbb N\n   \\]\n\n   be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) lines that appear in a configuration and put  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)                                   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (the number of sunny lines).\n\n2. **Necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The three lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(1,n)\n   \\]\n\n   belong to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them.  \n   Hence at least two of the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) must be non\u2011empty, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   H+V+D\\ge 2 .                                   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)\\le n-2 .                            \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k\\le n-2\\) is necessary.\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:\\;x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}^{(j)}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(1+(n-k-1)=n-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-2t-1).                 \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{t}\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   We shall prove that the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{0},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}\\) together with the lines introduced above cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   ----  \n\n   **Lemma 1.** *Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) satisfy \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  \n   Then there exists a unique integer \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b\\le n+1-a . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The hypotheses give  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a .                               \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting \\(a\\) from (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\le b-a\\le n+1-2a .                           \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(n+1-2a\\le n-3\\).  \n   Moreover, from \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .                             \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(a\\le k+1\\) and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\ge n-k-(k+1)=n-2k-1 .                        \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (7), (8) and (9) we obtain the double inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2k-1\\;\\le\\;b-a\\;\\le\\;n-3 .                      \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side of (10) is at most \\(n-3\\); the left\u2011hand side is at least \\(n-2k-1\\).  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{\\,n-2t-1\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}\n   =\\{\\,n-1,\\;n-3,\\;n-5,\\dots ,n-2k+1\\,\\}            \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly the \\(k\\) odd integers that lie in the interval \\([\\,n-2k-1,\\;n-1\\,]\\).  \n   Because (10) tells us that \\(b-a\\) is an odd integer belonging to the same interval, there is a **unique** \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   b-a = n-2t-1 .                                    \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (12) is precisely the equation of the line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) given in (4).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\), and the uniqueness of \\(t\\) shows that it lies on **exactly one** sunny line. \u220e\n\n   ----  \n\n   **Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) is covered.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   * If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on one of the lines \\(L_{h}^{(j)}\\).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), i.e. on \\(L_{v}\\).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) then Lemma\u202f1 applies and \\((a,b)\\) lies on a unique sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\).\n\n   Thus every lattice point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   Because the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for all such \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
