e core of the solution. It consists of:\n\n- One vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n- Horizontal lines \\(y=j\\) for \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-k-1\\).\n- Sunny lines \\(\\ell_{t}: y = x + (n-2t-1)\\) for \\(t=0,\\dots ,k-1\\).\n\nThe count of lines is indeed \\(1+(n-k-1)+k = n\\). The vertical and horizontal lines are non‑sunny, and the \\(\\ell_{t}\\) have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny. So the counting is correct.\n\nThe crucial claim is that **every** point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of these lines. The solution splits the points into three cases, the third case relying on Lemma 1.\n\n#### 4.1 Lemma 1\n\n**Statement:** For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k\\) there is a unique \\(t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).\n\n**Verification of the proof:**\n\n1. From \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) → (5) \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). *Correct.*\n\n2. Combining with \\(b\\ge n-k\\) gives (6) \\(n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a\\). *Correct.*\n\n3. Subtracting \\(a\\) yields (7) \\(n-k-a\\le b-a\\le n+1-2a\\). *Correct.*\n\n4. Since \\(a\\ge2\\), the right‑hand side satisfies \\(n+1-2a\\le n-3\\). *Correct.*\n\n5. From \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and (5) they deduce \\(a\\le k+1\\) (8). Indeed,\n   \\(n-k\\le n+1-a\\) ⇒ \\(a\\le k+1\\). *Correct.*\n\n6. Using (8) they obtain (9) \\(n-k-a\\ge n-2k-1\\). *Correct.*\n\n7. Combining (7) and (9) gives (10) \\(n-2k-1\\le b-a\\le n-3\\). *Correct.*\n\n8. They then claim that the set  \n   \\(S=\\{n-2t-1\\mid t=0,\\dots ,k-1\\} = \\{n-1, n-3, \\dots , n-2k+1\\}\\)  \n   consists of exactly the \\(k\\) **odd** integers in the interval \\([\\,n-2k-1,\\,n-1\\,]\\).  \n\n   **Error (Justification Gap):**  \n   The parity of the numbers in \\(S\\) is the same as the parity of \\(n-1\\). If \\(n\\) is even, the elements of \\(S\\) are odd; if \\(n\\) is odd, they are even. The proof does **not** establish that \\(b-a\\) has this parity. Consequently the claim that “\