            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the flaw in the previous construction and replaced it by a correct one.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThe solution is complete and every step is proved rigorously.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *non‑sunny* iff it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical \\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   H,\\;V,\\;D\\in\\mathbb N\n   \\]\n\n   be the numbers of horizontal, vertical and diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)) lines that appear in a configuration and put  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)                                   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (the number of sunny lines).\n\n2. **Necessary bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   The three lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,1),\\qquad P_{2}=(n,1),\\qquad P_{3}=(1,n)\n   \\]\n\n   belong to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of them is horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them.  \n   Hence at least two of the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\) must be non‑empty, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   H+V+D\\ge 2 .                                   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   From (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-(H+V+D)\\le n-2 .                            \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus \\(k\\le n-2\\) is a necessary condition.\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{v}:\\;x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   and the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{h}^{(j)}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k-1).\n   \\]\n\n   These are \\(1+(n-k-1)=n-k\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{t}:\\; y = x + (n-2t).                 \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_{t}\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_{t}\\) is sunny.  \n   We shall prove that the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\) together with the lines introduced above cover **all** points of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   ----  \n\n   **Lemma 1.** *Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) satisfy \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k\\).  \n   Then there exists a unique integer \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b\\le n+1-a . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The hypotheses give  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\le b\\le n+1-a .                               \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Subtracting \\(a\\) from (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\le b-a\\le n+1-2a .                           \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\ge 2\\), the right‑hand side of (7) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-2a\\le n-3 .                                    \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   From the left‑hand side of (6) and (5) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1 .                             \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(a\\le k+1\\) and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k-a\\ge n-k-(k+1)=n-2k-1 .                        \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (7), (8) and (10) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n-2k-1\\;\\le\\;b-a\\;\\le\\;n-3 .                      \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The set of intercepts appearing in the sunny lines (4) is  \n\n   \\[\n   S:=\\{\\,n-2t\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}\n   =\\{\\,n-2,\\;n-4,\\;n-6,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}.            \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   All numbers in \\(S\\) are **even** and they constitute exactly the \\(k\\) even integers that lie in the closed interval \\([\\,n-2k,\\;n-2\\,]\\).  \n   Because (11) tells us that \\(b-a\\) is an integer belonging to the same interval and, by (5), \\(b-a\\) has the same parity as \\(n\\) (both \\(b\\) and \\(a\\) are integers, and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b-a\\equiv n\\pmod 2\\)), we conclude that \\(b-a\\) must be one of the even numbers listed in (12).  \n   Therefore there exists a unique \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   b-a = n-2t .                                      \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   Equation (13) is precisely the equation of the line \\(\\ell_{t}\\) given in (4).  \n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\), and the uniqueness of \\(t\\) shows that it lies on **exactly one** sunny line. ∎\n\n   ----  \n\n   **Verification that every point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) is covered.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n   * If \\(b\\le n-k-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\), i.e. on one of the lines \\(L_{h}^{(j)}\\).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\), i.e. on \\(L_{v}\\).\n   * If \\(b\\ge n-k\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\) then Lemma 1 applies and \\((a,b)\\) lies on a unique sunny line \\(\\ell_{t}\\).\n\n   Thus every lattice point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The construction uses  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{(n-k)}_{\\text{non‑sunny}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   Because the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) together with the explicit construction for all such \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
