e point. The analogous statements hold for lines parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (at most one point per row) and for lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (at most one point per diagonal). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f3  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines are sunny, then at most \\(2n\\) points of \\(B_n\\) can be covered.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most two distinct points of \\(B_n\\). With \\(n\\) sunny lines the total number of covered border points is therefore at most \\(2n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f4  \nIf at most one of the \\(n\\) lines is non\u2011sunny, then the interior set \\(I_n\\) cannot be completely covered.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume exactly one line \\(\\ell_0\\) is non\u2011sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Covering of border points.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line covers at most two points of \\(B_n\\); the non\u2011sunny line, by Lemma\u202f2, can meet \\(B_n\\) in at most one point (otherwise it would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence the total number of border points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)+1 = 2n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|B_n|=n\\) and \\(2n-1\\ge n\\) for all \\(n\\ge 1\\), the border points can indeed be covered; this imposes no contradiction.\n\n*Covering of interior points.*  \nConsider the family of rows \\(\\mathcal R=\\{y=b\\mid 1\\le b\\le n-1\\}\\).  \nFor each row \\(y=b\\) the set \\(I_n\\) contains exactly \\(b\\) interior points:\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n-b\\}.\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is not horizontal, so it meets each row in at most one point.  \nThe single non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell_0\\) can be horizontal, vertical, or diagonal:\n\n* If \\(\\ell_0\\) is horizontal, it lies in a single row and can cover at most \\(n-b\\) points of that row, but it contributes **zero** points to any other row.\n* If \\(\\ell_0\\) is vertical, it mee