g the hypothesis.\n\nBoth cases are impossible; therefore \\(k\\le n-2\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \nAny admissible configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines. Hence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe converse direction\u2014showing that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realized\u2014remains open. Completing that part would give a full solution to the original problem."
