equality holding because \\(a\\le 1\\) gives \\(t\\ge 1\\) directly, while for \\(a\\ge 2\\) we have \\(2-a\\le 0\\) and the inequality is still true.\n\nA simpler argument: \\(b\\ge 1\\) implies \\(b-a+1\\ge 1-a+1=2-a\\). Since \\(a\\ge 1\\), \\(2-a\\ge 1\\) when \\(a=1\\) and is \\(\\le 1\\) otherwise; in any case \\(t\\ge 1\\).\n\n*Upper bound.*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb-a+1\\le (n+1-a)-a+1 = n+2-2a\\le n,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a\\ge 1\\) gives \\(2a\\ge 2\\) and thus \\(n+2-2a\\le n\\). Hence \\(t\\le n\\).\n\nThus \\(t\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). Substituting \\(t\\) into (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nb = a + (1-t) \\iff b-a = 1-t \\iff t = b-a+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_{t}\\). ∎\n\nFrom the lemma we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{t=1}^{n} S_{t}=T_{n}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Keeping exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nRetain the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_{1},S_{2},\\dots ,S_{k}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_{n}\\) with  \n\n\\[\n-(k-1)\\le b-a\\le k-1 \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nare already covered by (3) because for such a point \\(t=b-a+1\\) lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\nThe points that may remain uncovered are those for which  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\quad\\text{or}\\quad a-b\\ge k. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Geometry of the uncovered points\n\nAssume \\(b-a\\ge k\\). Adding the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, from \\(a-b\\ge k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInequalities (6)–(7) imply that every uncovered point satisfies either \\(a\\le m\\) or \\(b\\le m\\). Hence all uncovered points lie in the union of the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=m . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Adding non‑sunny lines\n\nLines (9) and (10) are parallel to the coordinate axes, therefore they are **non‑sunny**.  \nBecause \\(m\\le n-k\\) (indeed \\(2m\\le n+1-k\\) by definition of \\(m\\)), we can select exactly \\(n-k\\) distinct lines among (9)–(10). A convenient choice is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{x=1,\\dots ,x=m}_{v=m\\text{ vertical lines}}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\underbrace{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k-m}_{h=n-k-m\\text{ horizontal lines}} .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus we have added \\(v+h=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nEvery uncovered point of \\(T_{n}\\) has either its \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(\\le m\\) (hence lies on one of the vertical lines) or its \\(y\\)-coordinate \\(\\le m\\) (hence lies on one of the horizontal lines). Consequently, together with the sunny lines (3) all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Verification of the construction\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nWe use the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) and the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (11), for a total of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nOnly the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\) have slope \\(1\\); all other lines are parallel to the axes, hence are not sunny. Therefore exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nBy Lemma (2) the family \\(\\{S_{t}\\}_{t=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_{n}\\). Removing the sunny lines with indices \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) leaves uncovered precisely those points satisfying (5). As shown in Sections 4–5 those points are all captured by the vertical/horizontal lines (11). Hence every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of the constructed family.\n\nThus for the arbitrary integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have produced a valid configuration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Exhaustion of possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nBecause we must have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number of sunny lines cannot be larger than \\(n\\) nor negative. The construction above demonstrates that **every** integer \\(k\\) in the interval \\([0,n]\\) can be realized. Consequently no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two given conditions is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
