            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Sunny lines of slope 1\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}:\\; y = x + d . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_{d}\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{d}\\) exactly when \\(b-a=d\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lemma: every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on some \\(L_{d}\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) then \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n-(n-1)\\le b-a \\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(d:=b-a\\) belongs to the integer interval\n\\(\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n\\(L_{d}\\). ∎\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{d=-(n-1)}^{\\,n-1} L_{d}=T_{n}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for a prescribed \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{k}= \\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct because their intercepts \\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\) differ.\n\n*Uncovered points after \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) is not on any line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) then\n\\(b-a\\ge k\\). Using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a \\le n+1-k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\na\\le m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nConsider the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{k}= \\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThey are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are all\ndifferent because the abscissae are distinct.\n\nSince \\(2m\\le n+1-k\\), we have \\(m\\le n-k\\); therefore every integer\n\\(a\\) satisfying (4) is among \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) and the point\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}= \\mathcal S_{k}\\cup\\mathcal V_{k}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Verification of the construction\n\n*Number of lines.*  \n\\(|\\mathcal S_{k}|=k\\) and \\(|\\mathcal V_{k}|=n-k\\); hence\n\\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \nOnly the lines in \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) have slope \\(1\\); all lines in\n\\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\) are vertical and therefore not sunny.  Consequently\nexactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nBy Lemma 2 every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on some line \\(L_{d}\\) with\n\\(d\\in\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nIf \\(d\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the point is on a line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\).\nIf \\(d\\ge k\\) then (4) holds, and the point is on a vertical line of\n\\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_{n}\\) are covered by\n\\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Exhaustion of possible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nBecause we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot\nexceed \\(n\\) nor be negative.  Therefore the set of admissible values\nof \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
