            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(-(n-1)\\le b-a\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) we obtain \\(b-a\\ge -(a-1)\\ge -(n-1)\\) because\n\\(a\\le n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \nSimilarly \\(b-a\\le b-1\\le n-a\\le n-1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (family of sunny lines)  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{d}:\\; y = x + d .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_{d}\\) are sunny, and a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{d}\\) iff\n\\(b-a=d\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The slope of \\(L_{d}\\) is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); the equation\n\\(b-a=d\\) is equivalent to \\(b = a+d\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (coverage by the \\(L_{d}\\))  \nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to exactly one line \\(L_{d}\\) with\n\\(d\\in\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  By Lemma 1 the difference \\(d=b-a\\) lies in that interval, and by\nLemma 2 the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_{d}\\). Uniqueness follows\nfrom the fact that a point determines its difference uniquely. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_{k}= \\{L_{-(k-1)},L_{-(k-2)},\\dots ,L_{0}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their intercepts are distinct.\n\n2. **Uncovered points.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) be not on any line of \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\).  \n   Then its difference \\(d=b-a\\) cannot belong to\n   \\(\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,0\\}\\); hence \\(d\\ge 1\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(2a+d\\le n+1\\), i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-d}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the smallest positive difference not represented in\n   \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\) is \\(d=k\\), inequality (5) yields the uniform bound  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le r:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Vertical lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal V_{k}= \\{x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=r\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore non‑sunny, and are\n   pairwise distinct.\n\n   For any uncovered point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(a\\le r\\) by (6), so the\n   point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\).\n\n4. **Completing to \\(n\\) lines.**  \n   The set \\(\\mathcal S_{k}\\cup\\mathcal V_{k}\\) contains \\(k+r\\) lines.\n   Since \\(r\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\), we have \\(k+r\\le n\\).  \n   Add arbitrary \\((n-k-r)\\) further vertical lines with distinct\n   abscissae larger than \\(r\\) (for instance \\(x=r+1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\)).\n   Denote the resulting family of vertical lines by \\(\\mathcal V'_{k}\\); it\n   has exactly \\(n-k\\) members, all non‑sunny.\n\n   Finally set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}= \\mathcal S_{k}\\cup\\mathcal V'_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=k+(n-k)=n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n   and by the discussion above every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on at least\n   one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustion of possible \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nConversely, we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines because we are\nrestricted to \\(n\\) distinct lines, and we cannot have a negative number\nof sunny lines.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
