n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set to be covered\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\) (including the boundary).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nOur family of \\(n\\) distinct lines must cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  How many points of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a single line?\n\n#### Lemma 2.1 (sunny lines).  \nA sunny line meets \\(T_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).  \nSince the three points are collinear, the slopes of the segments \\((a_i,b_i)-(a_j,b_j)\\) are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_i-b_j}{a_i-a_j}=m\\qquad\\text{for all }i\\neq j,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(m\\) is the slope of \\(\\ell\\).  \nAll differences \\(a_i-a_j\\) and \\(b_i-b_j\\) are integers whose absolute values are at most \\(n-1\\).  \nIf \\(m\\) were not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), the three points could not all satisfy the linear equation with integer coefficients because the only integer slopes that keep the sum \\(a+b\\) constant (or keep one coordinate constant) are precisely \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nHence a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2 (non\u2011sunny lines).  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 1\\): points of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy \\(b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) such points.  \n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \n\n*Line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge 2\\).  \nPoints of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfy \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\); the number of solutions is \\(c-1\\le n\\) because \\(c\\le n+1\\) for any point of \\(T_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\nFrom Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2 we have the following bounds, which will be used repeatedly:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Sunny line } \\Rightarrow \\le 2 \\text{ points of }T_n},\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\text{Non\u2011sunny line } \\Rightarrow \\le n \\text{ points of }T_n}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and by \\(n-k\\) the number of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nUsing (2),\n\n* the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points,\n* the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at most \\(n(n-k)\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered,  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-(n-2)k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) from (1) into (3):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\le n^{2}-(n-2)k\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n(n-2)k\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge 3\\), \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}< (n-2)n\\); dividing by the positive integer \\(n-2\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus **any feasible configuration must satisfy**  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Constructions attaining all values in \\([0,n-2]\\)\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can actually be realized.\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny)\n\nTake the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n-2\\)   \\((n-2\\) lines);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* the line \\(x+y=n\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll these lines are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have three possibilities:\n\n1. If \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n2. If \\(a=n-1\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have \\(b=1\\); the point lies on \\(y=1\\).\n3. If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\); the point satisfies \\(x+y=n\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and no line is sunny.  \nThus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=n-2\\) (maximum number of sunny lines)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{1-i}{i-1}\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny and contains exactly the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\).\n\nThe two points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\).  \nCover them with the two non\u2011sunny lines\n\n* \\(x=n-1\\) (vertical) and\n* \\(y=n-1\\) (horizontal).\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(n-2\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them.  \nThus \\(k=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n#### 4.3  Obtaining intermediate values\n\nStarting from the configuration of \u00a74.2 (which has \\(k=n-2\\)), we can **reduce** the number of sunny lines one by one while preserving the covering property.\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) replace the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (or, equivalently, by the horizontal line \\(y=i\\)).  \nBoth replacement lines are non\u2011sunny and contain the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\) that \\(\\ell_i\\) covered, because\n\n\\[\n(i,1)\\in\\{x=i\\},\\qquad (1,i)\\in\\{y=i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of points covered does not change, while the number of sunny lines decreases by exactly one.\n\nRepeating this replacement for any collection of \\(t\\) distinct indices \\(i\\) yields a configuration with  \n\n\\[\nk=(n-2)-t\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  \nSince \\(t