            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\cup\\{n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nIn other words, one can arrange \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) so that the number of sunny lines is any integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\) inclusive, or exactly \\(n\\).  \nNo arrangement exists with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let \\(h,v,d\\) be the numbers of lines parallel respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis and the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines. Then  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+d+k=n,\\qquad h,v,d,k\\ge 0. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Necessary inequality.**  \n   Examine the three “border” sets of points  \n\n   \\[\n   B_h=\\{(a,1):1\\le a\\le n\\},\\;\n   B_v=\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\},\\;\n   B_d=\\{(a,n+1-a):1\\le a\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   * A point of \\(B_h\\) can be covered only by a horizontal line or by a sunny line (the diagonal line never meets \\(y=1\\)). Hence each point of \\(B_h\\) forces a distinct horizontal or sunny line, giving  \n\n     \\[\n     h+k\\ge n. \\tag{2}\n     \\]\n\n   * By symmetry, points of \\(B_v\\) give  \n\n     \\[\n     v+k\\ge n. \\tag{3}\n     \\]\n\n   * A point of \\(B_d\\) cannot lie on a horizontal or vertical line, so it must be covered either by the diagonal line or by a sunny line, whence  \n\n     \\[\n     d+k\\ge n. \\tag{4}\n     \\]\n\n   Adding (2)–(4) and using (1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-k)+3k\\ge 3n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\ge n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k=n. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\cup\\{n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Excluding \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   If \\(k=n-1\\) then (2)–(4) force \\(h=v=d=1\\).  \n   A line cannot be simultaneously horizontal, vertical and diagonal, so such a configuration cannot exist. Hence \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible, confirming (5).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny).**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{1}{i+1}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(L_i\\) are distinct.  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) and set \\(i:=a+b-1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(i\\) into (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b=\\frac{i}{i+1}a+\\frac{1}{i+1}\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   (i+1)b=ia+1\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   ib+ b=ia+1\n   \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   i(b-a)=1-b .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(i=a+b-1\\) the last equality holds identically, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a+b-1}\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and provides a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for any \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Fix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\); we shall realise \\(k=t\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   h=n-t-2\\quad\\text{horizontal lines }y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=h,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   v=n-t-2\\quad\\text{vertical lines }x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=v,\n   \\]\n   and one diagonal line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Thus we have \\(h+v+1=n-t\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   Take the first \\(t\\) lines of the family (6):\n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+\\frac{1}{i+1}\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,t).\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are sunny and none coincides with any of the horizontal, vertical or diagonal lines already placed.\n\n   *Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered.*\n\n   - **Case 1.** \\(a\\le v\\). Then the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n   - **Case 2.** \\(b\\le h\\). Then the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n   - **Case 3.** \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\).  \n     Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(v=h=n-t-2\\), we have  \n\n     \\[\n     a+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a+b-1\\le n-t-1=t+(n-t-1)-t\\le t .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(i:=a+b-1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le i\\le t\\), and by the argument of Section 4 the point belongs to the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   The three cases exhaust all possibilities, so every required lattice point is on at least one of the constructed lines.  \n   The total number of distinct lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   h+v+1+t=(n-t-2)+(n-t-2)+1+t=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly \\(t\\) of them are sunny.  \n   Therefore for each \\(t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) a configuration with \\(k=t\\) exists.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (5) together with the explicit constructions for all admissible values shows that the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\cup\\{n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n   No configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can exist, and every other non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can be attained. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In yo