ne (i.e. by a line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll other diagonals are each covered completely by a distinct non‑sunny line, because a line of slope \\(-1\\) can contain points from only one diagonal.\n\nEach uncovered diagonal \\(D_s\\) contains at least two points, except possibly \\(D_2\\) which contains exactly one point.  \nBy Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most one point to any given diagonal, hence the total number of points of the uncovered diagonals that can be supplied by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is at most \\(k\\cdot r\\).\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{D_s\\text{ uncovered}}|D_s|\\le k r . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r\\ge 2\\) then the left‑hand side of (5) is at least \\(2r\\) (since at least two uncovered diagonals have size \\(\\ge 2\\)), while the right‑hand side equals \\(k r\\).  \nFor any \\(k\\ge 1\\) this forces \\(2\\le k\\), and equality would require every uncovered diagonal to have size \\(1\\), impossible when \\(r\\ge 2\\).  \nHence \\(r\\le 1\\).\n\nIf \\(r=1\\) then the sole uncovered diagonal must be \\(D_2\\); otherwise its size would be at least \\(2\\) and (5) would give \\(2\\le k\\), contradicting the fact that we have only \\(k\\) sunny lines to cover the single point of \\(D_2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 1 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\; y=-x+s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are not sunny), and each line \\(L_s\\) contains exactly the diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 2 (Existence for \\(k=1\\))\n\nReplace the line \\(L_2\\) in (6) by any sunny line passing through the unique point of \\(D_2\\); for concreteness take  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y = x .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}_1=\\{S\\}\\cup\\{L_s\\mid s=3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(S\\)) is sunny, and it still covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\), all other points lie on the appropriate \\(L_s\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Proposition 3 (Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 2 at most one diagonal can be uncovered by a non‑sunny line, and that diagonal would have to be \\(D_2\\).  \nBut covering \\(D_2\\) already consumes **one** sunny line (it contains a single point).  \nThe remaining \\(k-1\\ge 1\\) sunny lines would then have to cover points of the other diagonals that are not already covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \nSince every other diagonal has at least two points, Lemma 1 shows that a single sunny line can contribute at most one point to each such diagonal, leaving at least one point uncovered on each of those diagonals.  \nThus the covering condition cannot be satisfied.  \nHence no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth values are attainable by the explicit constructions given in Propositions 1 and 2, and Proposition 3 proves that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
