            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|D_s|=s-1,\\qquad \nT_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{\\,n+1} D_s,\\qquad \nD_s\\cap D_{s'}=\\varnothing\\;(s\\neq s'). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is **not** \\(-1\\) (this includes every sunny line, every horizontal line and every vertical line). Then \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_s\\).  \nBecause both belong to the same diagonal, \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a_2\\neq a_1\\) the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nIf \\(a_2=a_1\\) then also \\(b_2=b_1\\) (since the sum \\(a+b\\) is fixed), contradicting the distinctness of the two points.  \nThus no such pair exists and \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_s\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) meets exactly one diagonal, and for each \\(s\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=-x+s\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely the diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\) then \\(b=-a+s\\), i.e. \\(a+b=s\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).  \nConversely, any \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) satisfies \\(b=s-a\\), i.e. lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell_s\\cap T_n=D_s\\).  Because the slope is \\(-1\\), Lemma 1 shows that \\(\\ell_s\\) cannot meet any other diagonal. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_s\\;(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \nAll have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are not sunny) and each covers the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nBy (3) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these lines, and the family contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the line covering the smallest diagonal \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for definiteness use  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_1=\\{S\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(S\\)) is sunny.  \n\\(S\\) contains the point \\((1,1)\\); the remaining diagonals are covered by the corresponding \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal F\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) lines cover \\(t\\) whole diagonals (Lemma 2).  \nThe remaining \\(r:=n-t\\) lines are either sunny or have slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\); by Lemma 1 each of them meets any diagonal in at most one point.\n\nBecause we would always use the slope‑\\(-1\\) lines for the *largest* diagonals (they contain the most points), the uncovered diagonals are precisely the \\(r\\) smallest ones:\n\n\\[\nD_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{r+1}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of points that must be supplied by the \\(r\\) non‑\\(-1\\) lines is\n\n\\[\nS=\\sum_{s=2}^{r+1}|D_s|\n   =\\sum_{s=2}^{r+1}(s-1)\n   =\\frac{r(r+1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach of the \\(r\\) lines can contribute at most one point to each diagonal, so the maximal number of points that the \\(r\\) lines can collectively provide is \\(r\\cdot r=r^{2}\\).  Hence we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{r(r+1)}{2}\\le r^{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad r\\le 2. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus at most two diagonals can be left uncovered.\n\n*Case \\(r=0\\).*  \nAll diagonals are covered by slope‑\\(-1\\) lines, so no sunny line occurs: \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Case \\(r=1\\).*  \nThe only uncovered diagonal is \\(D_2\\) (size 1).  It can be covered by a single sunny line, giving \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Case \\(r=2\\).*  \nThe uncovered diagonals are \\(D_2\\) and \\(D_3\\), containing respectively \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) points.  \nBecause a line that is not of slope \\(-1\\) cannot contain two distinct points of the same diagonal (Lemma 1), the two points of \\(D_3\\) must lie on **different** lines.  Consequently at least three distinct lines are needed to cover the three points \\(\\{(1,1)\\}\\cup D_3\\).  However we have only \\(r=2\\) lines that are not of slope \\(-1\\); among them exactly \\(k\\) are sunny, and the remaining (if any) have slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  In any event the total number of such lines is \\(2\\), which is insufficient to cover the three required points.  Hence the situation \\(r=2\\) cannot occur when \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\nSince the only possibilities for \\(r\\) are \\(0,1,2\\) and the cases \\(r=0,1\\) correspond to \\(k=0,1\\) respectively, we conclude that no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}.\n\\]\n\nBoth values are realizable by the explicit constructions given above, and the counting argument shows that larger values of \\(k\\) are impossible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
