The two non\u2011\\(-1\\) lines are exactly the lines that are not of slope \\(-1\\); they may be sunny, horizontal or vertical.\n\n- By Lemma\u202f1 a line that is not of slope \\(-1\\) cannot contain two distinct points of the same diagonal.  \n- Hence the two points of \\(D_3\\) must lie on **different** lines.  \n- The single point of \\(D_2\\) cannot lie on either of those two lines, because that would give a line containing two points belonging to the same diagonal (\\(D_2\\) together with one point of \\(D_3\\) would force slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the line\u2019s type).  \n\nThus at least three distinct non\u2011\\(-1\\) lines are required to cover the three points \\(\\{(1,1)\\}\\cup D_3\\), contradicting the fact that only \\(r=2\\) such lines exist.  Therefore the case \\(r=2\\) is impossible.\n\nFrom (4) we have shown that no other value of \\(r\\) can occur.  Consequently the only feasible configurations are those with \\(r=0\\) (giving \\(k=0\\)) or \\(r=1\\) (giving \\(k=1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer\n\nBoth values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable by the explicit constructions given above, and the arguments above prove that no configuration with \\(k\\ge2\\) exists.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\text{ or }k=1}\n\\]"
