g{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is **not** \\(-1\\) (this includes every sunny line, every horizontal line and every vertical line). Then \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(\\ell\\) contains two distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in D_s\\).  \nBecause both belong to the same diagonal, \\(a_1+b_1=a_2+b_2=s\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nb_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a_2\\neq a_1\\) the slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}=-1\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nIf \\(a_2=a_1\\) then also \\(b_2=b_1\\) (the sum \\(a+b\\) is fixed), contradicting distinctness.  \nThus no such pair exists and \\(\\ell\\) meets \\(D_s\\) in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) meets exactly one diagonal, and for each \\(s\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=-x+s\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely the diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_s\\) then \\(b=-a+s\\), i.e. \\(a+b=s\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\).  \nConversely, any \\((a,b)\\in D_s\\) satisfies \\(b=s-a\\), i.e. lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell_s\\cap T_n=D_s\\).  Because the slope is \\(-1\\), Lemma 1 shows that \\(\\ell_s\\) cannot meet any other diagonal. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_s\\;(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\\).  \nAll have slope \\(-1\\) (hence are not sunny) and each covers the whole diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \nBy (1) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of these lines, and the family contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the line covering the smallest diagonal \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for definiteness use  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_1=\\{S\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_s\\mid s=3,4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(S\\)) is sunny.  \n\\(S\\) contains the point \\((1,1)\\); the remaining diagonals are covered by the corresponding \\(\\ell_s\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### General structure of a covering family\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nt:=\\#\\{\\text{lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal F\\},\\qquad\nr:=n-t .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(t\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\) each cover a whole diagonal (Lemma 2).  \nThe remaining \\(r\\) lines are not of slope \\(-1\\); by Lemma 1 each of them meets any diagonal in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Claim 1 (placement of the \\(-1\\) lines)\n\nIn a configuration that uses the maximal possible number of \\(-1\\)‑slope lines, those lines cover the **largest** diagonals, namely  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1},D_{n},\\dots ,D_{n-t+2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume contrary that some diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\) not among the \\(t\\) largest is covered by a \\(-1\\) line while a larger diagonal \\(D_{s'}\\) (\\(s'>s\\)) is left to be covered by a non‑\\(-1\\) line.  \nBecause a non‑\\(-1\\) line can contribute at most one point to \\(D_{s'}\\) (Lemma 1) but \\(|D_{s'}|=s'-1\\ge 2\\), the point(s) of \\(D_{s'}\\) not on that line would have to be supplied by other lines, increasing the total number of lines needed.  \nInstead, replace the \\(-1\\) line on \\(D_s\\) by a non‑\\(-1\\) line (possible because \\(D_s\\) has only one point when \\(s=2\\) and otherwise a non‑\\(-1\\) line can cover at most one of its points, which is suff