e points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate exceeds \\(s\\).\n\n   - **Verification of covering:**  \n     * Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le s\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\) (by construction).  \n     * Every point with \\(a> s\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_{a-s}\\).  \n     Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   - The construction uses exactly \\(s=k\\) sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, and all lines are distinct.  \n\n3. **Conclusion:**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) this proves that the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); these are exactly the lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).  \nSince we are allowed to use at most \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(k\\) satisfying (1)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\ns:=k,\\qquad t:=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall construct \\(s\\) sunny lines and \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,s\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=(i+1)x-i(i+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Slope.*  The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i+1\\).  Since \\(i+1\\ge 2\\), it is different from \\(0