"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe only non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\) for which the required configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary condition.**  \n   Show that any line which is not vertical (i.e. not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) can contain at most one point among the set  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   Consequently, to cover all points of \\(S_n\\) we need a vertical line for every possible abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n2. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) cover \\(S_n\\) and none of them is sunny, giving a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 1\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with at least one non\u2011vertical line exists.  \n   Using the observation of step\u202f1, deduce that all vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) must still be present, which would already give \\(n\\) lines.  \n   Adding any non\u2011vertical line would exceed the allowed number \\(n\\).  \n   Hence no configuration can contain a sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\) cannot be positive.\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_n(a)=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}= \\{(a,b)\\mid b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\; b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|S_n(a)|=n+1-a\\ge 1\\); in particular each abscissa \\(a\\) occurs in at least one point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) is **not** vertical (i.e. its slope is finite), then \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of the set \\(S_n(a)\\) for any fixed \\(a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA non\u2011vertical line has an equation \\(y=mx+c\\) with a finite slope \\(m\\).  \nIf two distinct points \\((a,b_1)\\) and \\((a,b_2)\\) with the same abscissa \\(a\\) lay on \\(\\ell\\), then\n\n\\[\nb_1 = m a + c = b_2,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(b_1=b_2\\), contradicting the assumption that the points are distinct. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nTo cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\) with at most \\(n\\) distinct lines, every abscissa \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) must be represented by a **vertical** line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix an \\(a\\). The set \\(S_n(a)\\) contains at least one point, and by Lemma\u202f1 a non\u2011vertical line can contain at most one element of \\(S_n(a)\\).  \nIf we do **not** use the vertical line \\(x=a\\), then each point of \\(S_n(a)\\) would require a distinct non\u2011vertical line.  \nSince \\(|S_n(a)|=n+1-a\\ge 1\\), we would need at least \\(n+1-a\\) different lines for this single value of \\(a\\).  \nSumming over all \\(a\\) would give more than \\(n\\) lines, which is forbidden.  \nHence the only way to stay within the budget of \\(n\\) lines is to include the vertical line \\(x=a\\) for every \\(a\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_a:\\; x=a\\qquad (a=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_a\\) contains exactly the set \\(S_n(a)\\); therefore the union \\(\\bigcup_{a=1}^{n}\\ell_a\\) equals \\(S_n\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.  \nThus we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of any sunny line (\\(k\\ge 1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition contains at least one sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \nA sunny line is, by definition, **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); in particular it is **not vertical**.\n\nBy Lemma\u202f2, to cover all points of \\(S_n\\) we must have the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \nThese are already \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nAdding the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) would give at least \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, contradicting the requirement that exactly \\(n\\) lines be used.\n\nHence no configuration can contain a sunny line; i.e. the number of sunny lines must be \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) holds for any configuration.  \nThe construction above shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.  \nThe argument of Lemma\u202f2 together with the contradiction above proves that no configuration can have \\(k\\ge 1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}}.\n\\]"

