nted by a vertical line, all \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\) points of \\(C_a\\) must be supplied by the \\(s\\) sunny lines, so we need \\(n+1-a\\le s\\).  \n   Hence \\(a\\ge n+1-s\\).  \n   Consequently every omitted column satisfies (1), and there are exactly \\(s\\) of them because the total number of lines is \\(n\\). ∎\n\n4. **Counting points that must be covered by sunny lines.**  \n   By (1) the omitted columns are \\(C_{n-s+1},\\dots ,C_n\\).  \n   Their sizes form the decreasing sequence\n   \\[\n   s,\\;s-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;1,\n   \\]\n   so the total number of points that are *not* on a vertical line equals\n   \\[\n   \\Sigma_s:=1+2+\\dots +s=\\frac{s(s+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Capacity of sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line can meet each omitted column in at most one point (Lemma 1); therefore a single sunny line can cover at most \\(s\\) of the points in (2).  \n   With \\(s\\) sunny lines the maximal number of points they can collectively cover is \\(s\\cdot s=s^{2}\\).  \n   Since \\(\\Sigma_s\\le s^{2}\\) for every integer \\(s\\ge 0\\) (indeed \\(\\frac{s(s+1)}{2}\\le s^{2}\\) ⇔ \\(s\\ge 1\\)), the \\(s\\) sunny lines are *capable* of covering all points of the omitted columns.\n\n6. **Construction for every \\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – vertical lines.*  \n   Draw the vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-s .\n   \\]\n   They cover all points of the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-s}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – sunny lines.*  \n   For each omitted column \\(a\\in\\{n-s+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose a distinct point\n   \\[\n   P_a=(a,\\;n+1-a) \\in C_a .\n   \\]\n   For each \\(a\\) define a line \\(\\ell_a\\) that passes through \\(P_a\\) and has an irrational slope \\(m_a\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   m_a\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad m_a\\neq m_{a'}\\;(a\\neq a').\n   \\]\n   An explicit choice is\n   \\[\n   \\ell_a:\\; y=m_a(x-a)+ (n+1-a),\\qquad \n   m_a=\\sqrt{2}+a .\n   \\]\n   Because the slope is irrational, \\(\\ell_a\\) contains **no** other lattice point with positive integer coordinates; in particular it meets none of the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-s}\\) and meets each omitted column \\(C_{b}\\) in at most one point (namely \\(P_b\\) when \\(b=a\\)).  \n   Hence the set \\(\\{\\ell_{n-s+1},\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) consists of \\(s\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering exactly one point of \\(S_n\\), and together they cover all points listed in (2).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines (if its abscissa \\(\\le n-s\\)) or is one of the chosen points \\(P_a\\) (if its abscissa \\(>n-s\\)).  Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover all points required in the problem, and exactly \\(s\\) of them are sunny.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, Lemma 2 would force the set of omitted columns to have size \\(n\\); i.e. **every** column would be omitted.  Then (2) gives \\(\\Sigma_n=n(n+1)/2\\) points to be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  As each sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, a single sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points, so the \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(n^{2}\\) points.  Since\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\quad\\text{fails for }n\\ge3,\n   \\]\n   the inequality actually holds in the opposite direction:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^{2}\\quad\\text{for all }n\\ge2,\n   \\]\n   so the capacity argument does **not** rule out \\(k=n\\).  \n   However, with \\(n\\) sunny (hence non‑vertical) lines there are **no** vertical lines, so every column must be covered solely by the sunny lines.  Column \\(1\\) contains \\(n\\) points; to cover them we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct lines because a non‑vertical line can meet column 1 in at most one point.  Hence we would already need \\(n\\) lines just for column 1, leaving no line to cover any point of the other columns, which is impossible.  Therefore \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The constructions of step 6 show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  \n   The argument of step 7 shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  \n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
