o every point of that column must be covered by a sunny line. By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets such a column in at most one point; therefore we need at most \\(s\\) points in the column, i.e. \\(|C_a|=n+1-a\\le s\\). Hence \\(a\\ge n+1-s\\). Consequently the omitted columns are exactly those listed in (1), and there are precisely \\(s\\) of them. ∎  \n\n4. **Counting points that must be covered by sunny lines.**  \n   By (1) the omitted columns are \\(C_{n-s+1},\\dots ,C_n\\). Their sizes are\n   \\[\n   s,\\;s-1,\\;\\dots ,\\;1,\n   \\]\n   so the total number of points that are *not* on a vertical line equals\n   \\[\n   \\Sigma_s:=1+2+\\dots +s=\\frac{s(s+1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Capacity of sunny lines.**  \n   By Lemma 1 a sunny line can meet each omitted column in at most one point; therefore a single sunny line can cover at most \\(s\\) of the points counted in (2). With \\(s\\) sunny lines the maximal number of points they can collectively cover is \\(s\\cdot s=s^{2}\\). Since\n   \\[\n   \\frac{s(s+1)}{2}\\le s^{2}\\qquad (s\\ge 0),\n   \\]\n   the \\(s\\) sunny lines are *sufficient* to cover all points of the omitted columns.\n\n6. **Explicit construction for every \\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).**  \n\n   *Vertical lines.*  \n   Draw the vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-s .\n   \\]\n   They cover all points of the columns \\(C_{1},\\dots ,C_{n-s}\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   For each omitted column \\(a\\in\\{n-s+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) choose the **topmost** point of that column,\n   \\[\n   P_a=(a,\\;n+1-a)\\in C_a .\n   \\]\n   Define a line \\(\\ell_a\\) through \\(P_a\\) with an **irrational** slope \\(m_a\\) satisfying\n   \\[\n   m_a\\notin\\{0,-1\\},\\qquad m_a\\neq m_{a'}\\;(a\\neq a').\n   \\]\n   An explicit choice is\n   \\[\n   \\ell_a:\\; y=m_a(x-a)+(n+1-a),\\qquad m_a=\\sqrt{2}+a .\n   \\]\n\n   *Why the lines are sunny.*  \n   The slope \\(m_a\\) is irrational, hence \\(\\ell_a\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(m_a\\neq0\\)), not vertical, and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (which has slope \\(-1\\)). Thus each \\(\\ell_a\\) is sunny.\n\n   *Why each \\(\\ell_a\\) contains no other lattice point of \\(S_n\\).*  \n   Suppose \\(\\ell_a\\) contained another point \\((x',y')\\in S_n\\) with \\(x'\\neq a\\). Then\n   \\[\n   \\frac{y'- (n+1-a)}{x'-a}=m_a .\n   \\]\n   The left‑hand side is a ratio of two integers, hence rational, contradicting the irrationality of \\(m_a\\). Therefore \\(\\ell_a\\) meets \\(S_n\\) only at \\(P_a\\).\n\n   *Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   - If \\(a\\le n-s\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in step 1) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n   - If \\(a>n-s\\) then \\(a\\in\\{n-s+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). In column \\(C_a\\) the only point not already covered by a vertical line is the topmost point \\(P_a=(a,n+1-a)\\); all other points of the column have smaller \\(b\\) and are already covered by the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which does **not** exist because \\(a\\) is omitted). Hence the only point of \\(C_a\\) that needs covering is \\(P_a\\), and it lies on \\(\\ell_a\\) by construction.\n\n   Consequently all points of \\(S_n\\) belong to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, exactly \\(s\\) of which are sunny, and the lines are distinct. This shows that every integer \\(k=s\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n7. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Then there are **no** vertical lines. By Lemma 1 each sunny line meets a fixed column in at most one point. To cover column \\(C_a\\) (which contains \\(n+1-a\\) points) we would need at least \\(n+1-a\\) distinct sunny lines. Summing over all columns gives a lower bound on the number of distinct lines:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n   For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\). Hence at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) distinct sunny lines would be necessary, contradicting the hypothesis that only \\(n\\) lines are used. Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction of step 6 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can be realized, and step 7 proves that \\(k=n\\) is impossible. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
