(a\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\),\n\n\\[\na-b\\ge (n-k+1)-k = n-2k+1,\n\\]\nand because \\(b\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\na-b\\le a-1\\le n-1.\n\\]\n\nMoreover, using \\(b\\le k\\) we have the sharper upper bound  \n\n\\[\na-b\\le n+1-b-b = n+1-2b\\le n-1.\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\na-b\\in\\{\\,n-2k+1,\\;n-2k+2,\\dots ,\\,n-k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set on the right consists of exactly the \\(k\\) integers \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\) defined above. Hence there exists a unique index \\(i\\) with \\(c_i=a-b\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D_i\\) because \\(x-y=c_i\\) is satisfied.\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  \nExactly the \\(k\\) diagonal lines \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_k\\) are sunny, so the required configuration exists for this \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), all these values are admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  No other values of \\(k\\) are possible\n\nA line is *non‑sunny* precisely when it is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* horizontal direction \\((1,0)\\)  (slope \\(0\\));\n* vertical direction \\((0,1)\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* direction \\((1,-1)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nThus any non‑sunny line belongs to one of these three families.  \nAmong the \\(n\\) distinct lines we are allowed to choose, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny; equivalently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThe construction of §1.2 with \\(k=n\\) (take no vertical lines and the \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x-y=n-2n+i = -n+i\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\)) shows that the bound \\(k=n\\) is attainable. Hence the set of possible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the number of sunny lines that can appear in a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is precisely  \n\n\\