-------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  There are \\(k\\) lines of the form \\(L_i\\) and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines, so \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=n\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny; the vertical lines are not.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Distinctness.*  The slopes \\(\\frac{1}{i}\\) are pairwise different, so the lines \\(L_i\\) are distinct from each other and from any vertical line.  The vertical lines have distinct abscissae, so all lines in (5) are distinct.\n\n*Covering property.*  \nTake an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n1. **Case \\(a\\ge k+1\\).**  \n   The vertical line \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n2. **Case \\(a\\le k\\).**  \n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=a+1\\) or \\(b=a-1\\)*, then by (4) the point is one of the two lattice points lying on \\(L_a\\); hence it is covered.\n\n   *Otherwise* \\(b\\neq a\\pm1\\).  \n   From \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(a\\le k\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-k .\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(b\\le k\\) then \\(a+b\\le 2k\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=a\\pm1\\), contradicting the present sub\u2011case.  \n   Therefore \\(b\\ge k+1\\).  The vertical line \\(V_b\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) (because its index lies in \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)) and clearly contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness  \n\nFor any collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines the number of sunny lines is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nSection\u202f3 shows that **every** integer in this interval can be realised.  Consequently no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nAll non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nare attainable, and no other values occur.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
