\n   \\[\n   T:=\\bigcup_{d\\notin\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}} S_d .\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_a:\\;x=a .\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(a\\) is such that the column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is not\n   already contained in one of the sunny lines, then we keep the line \\(V_a\\);\n   otherwise we discard it.  \n   Because each column contains exactly the points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), and because for a fixed \\(a\\) the values of\n   \\(a-b\\) run through a consecutive interval of integers, **exactly one**\n   of those integers belongs to the chosen set \\(\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\).  \n   Consequently every column contains at least one point that lies on a sunny\n   line, and the remaining points of that column are all covered by the single\n   vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   The number of vertical lines that we keep is therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   All these vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are\n   non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.4 \u2013 Count the lines.*  \n   We have produced exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the chosen \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\)) and\n   \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines \\(V_a\\); altogether \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct\n   lines.  By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on one of the\n   sunny lines or on the appropriate vertical line, so the covering condition\n   is satisfied.\n\n4. **Conclusion** \u2013 the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). Together with the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) this\n   proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny.\nHence any admissible \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Extremal cases\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;y=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), therefore it lies on the line\n  \\(y=b\\).  All lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none is sunny.\n  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_i:\\;x-y=-n+i .\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n  For \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(-n+1\\le a-b\\le0\\); consequently there is a\n  unique \\(i\\) with \\(a-b=-n+i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  Hence the \\(n\\)\n  sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all points of \\(S_n\\), proving that\n  \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\n#### 3.1. Decomposition of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nd\\in\\{-n+1,-n+2,\\dots ,0\\}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nS_d=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(S_d\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nS_d\\subseteq\\ell_d,\\qquad \n\\ell_d:\\;x-y=d,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_d\\) is a line of slope \\(1\\).  \nA direct count shows  \n\n\\[\n|S_d|=n+d+1\\qquad(-n+1\\le d\\le0). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the whole set \\(S_n\\) is the disjoint union  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{d=-n+1}^{0} S_d .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2. Choice of sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nSelect any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nd_1<d_2<\\dots <d_k\\qquad\\text{with}\\qquad -n+1\\le d_i\\le0 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{d_i}:\\;x-y=d_i .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.  \nBy (3) the union of the sets \\(S_{d_i}\\) is covered by the sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.3. Covering the remaining points with vertical lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nT:=S_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k} S_{d_i}\n      =\\bigcup_{d\\notin\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}} S_d .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nIf the column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains a point of some\n\\(S_{d_i}\\) (i.e. if there exists \\(b\\) with \\(a-b=d_i\\) for some \\(i\\)), then that\npoint already lies on a sunny line and we **keep** the vertical line \\(V_a\\) to\ncover the *remaining* points of the column.  \nIf the column contains no point of any \\(S_{d_i}\\) we simply discard \\(V_a\\);\nin that case the whole column is empty, because the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\ge1\\) and thus \\(a\\le n\\).\n\nBecause for a fixed \\(a\\) the numbers \\(a-b\\) run through the consecutive\ninterval  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,a-(n+1-a),\\;a-(n-a),\\dots ,a-1\\,\\}\n   =\\{-n+2a-1,\\dots ,a-1\\},\n\\]\n\nexactly one element of this interval belongs to the chosen set\n\\(\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\).  Consequently every column contains **at most one**\npoint that is already covered by a sunny line, and all the other points of the\ncolumn are covered by the single vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nThe number of vertical lines that we keep equals the number of columns, i.e.\n\\(n\\), minus the number of columns whose unique point lies on a sunny line.\nSince each sunny line \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\) contains exactly one point in each column,\nthe total number of discarded vertical lines is precisely \\(k\\).  Hence we keep\n\n\\[\nn-k\n\\]\n\nvertical lines, all of which are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore\nnon\u201