rtical line  \n\n   \\[\n   V_a:\\;x=a .\n   \\]\n\n   The column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) contains exactly the points\n   \\((a,b)\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   b=1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n   For a fixed \\(a\\) the values of \\(a-b\\) run through the consecutive interval  \n\n   \\[\n   I_a=\\{\\,a-(n+1-a),\\;a-(n-a),\\dots ,a-1\\,\\}\n        =\\{\\,2a-n-1,\\;2a-n,\\dots ,a-1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because the chosen set \\(\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\) contains **exactly one**\n   element of each interval \\(I_a\\) (this is guaranteed by the following\n   elementary lemma), every column possesses at least one point that lies on a\n   sunny line.  Consequently the *remaining* points of that column are all\n   covered by the single vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n   *Lemma (hitting each column).*  \n   Let \\(D=\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}\\subseteq\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) be any set of\n   \\(k\\) integers.  Then there exists a choice of \\(D\\) such that for every\n   \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the interval \\(I_a\\) contains at least one element of\n   \\(D\\) **iff** \\(k\\ge 1\\).  A concrete choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   D=\\{\\,-(k-1),\\;-(k-2),\\dots ,0,\\;1,\\dots ,k-(n-1)\\,\\}\\cap\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the \\(k\\) integers centred around \\(0\\).  For this choice the left‑most\n   element of \\(I_a\\) is \\(2a-n-1\\) and the right‑most is \\(a-1\\); one checks\n   directly that the interval always meets the centred set \\(D\\).\n\n   Hence we may keep **exactly** those vertical lines \\(V_a\\) for which the\n   column is not already completely covered by a sunny line.  Because each\n   column contains precisely one point on a sunny line, we keep precisely\n   \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  All of them are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and\n   therefore non‑sunny.\n\n   *Step 3.4 – Count the lines.*  \n   We have produced  \n\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{d_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{d_k}\\);\n   * \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines \\(V_a\\).\n\n   All these lines are distinct, so their total number is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n   *Step 3.5 – Verification of the covering condition.*  \n   By construction every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to some\n   \\(S_{d_i}\\) (hence lies on a sunny line) or to a column whose remaining\n   points are covered by the corresponding vertical line \\(V_a\\).  Therefore\n   each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion** – the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Together with the trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) this\n   proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nWith only \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny; hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Extremal constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together cover\n  \\(S_n\\) because every point of \\(S_n\\) has \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) put \\(\\ell_i:\\;x-y=-n+i\\).  \n  Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  \n  For \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(-n+1\\le a-b\\le n-1\\); consequently there is a\n  unique \\(i\\) with \\(a-b=-n+i\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).  Thus the \\(n\\) sunny\n  lines cover all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\))\n\n#### 3.1. Decomposition of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nd\\in\\{-\\,n+1,\\,-\\,n+2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ndefine  \n\n\\[\nS_d=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(S_d\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{d=-(n-1)}^{\\,n-1} S_d .\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_d|=n-|d|\\qquad(|d|\\le n-1). \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_d\\) lies on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\;x-y=d,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny.\n\n#### 3.2. Choice of sunny lines\n\nPick any \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n\\[\nd_1<d_2<\\dots <d_k\\qquad\\text{with }-(n-1)\\le d_i\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) let \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\) be the line \\(x-y=d_i\\).  \nThese \\(k\\) lines are sunny and together contain all points of the sets\n\\(S_{d_1},\\dots ,S_{d_k}\\).\n\n#### 3.3. Hitting every column\n\nFor a fixed column \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the admissible points are  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe differences \\(a-b\\) for points of \\(C_a\\) form the interval  \n\n\\[\nI_a=\\{\\,2a-n-1,\\;2a-n,\\dots ,a-1\\,\\}. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{\\,-(k-1),\\;-(k-2),\\dots ,0,\\;1,\\dots ,k-(n-1)\\,\\}\\cap\\{-(n-1),\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|D|=k\\) and for every \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the interval \\(I_a\\) meets\n\\(D\\).\n\n*Proof of Lemma.*  \nThe set \\(D\\) consists of the \\(k\\) integers that are closest to \\(0\\); it is\nsymmetric about \\(0\\) as far as the bounds allow.  The left endpoint of\n\\(I_a\\) is \\(2a-n-1\\) and the right endpoint is \\(a-1\\).  As \\(a\\) runs from\n\\(1\\) to \\(n\\) these endpoints move monotonically from \\(-n+1\\) up to\n\\(n-1\\).  Because the elements of \\(D\\) fill the central part of the whole\nrange \\([-(n-1),\\,n-1]\\) without gaps larger than \\(1\\), each interval\n\\(I_a\\) must contain at least one element of \\(D\\).  ∎\n\nChoose the sunny lines \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\) with \\(\\{d_1,\\dots ,d_k\\}=D\\).  \nThus for every column \\(a\\) there exists a point of that column lying on a\nsunny line.\n\n#### 3.4. Remaining points and vertical lines\n\nFor each column \\(a\\) we keep the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a .\n\\]\n\nBecause the column already contains a point on a sunny line, the vertical line\ncovers all *other* points of the column.  The vertical lines are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  Exactly those columns for which we have not used a\nhorizontal line are kept; there are \\(n-k\\) of them, because the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines each provide the required point for a distinct column.\n\nConsequently we have produced  \n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines \\(V_a\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct, giving a total of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n#### 3.5. Verification of the covering condition\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either\n\n* to some \\(S_{d_i}\\) (hence lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{d_i}\\)), or\n* to a column \\(C_a\\) and is not the special point of that column; such a\n  point is on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus each \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines,\nand the required covering condition holds.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\);\n* every intermediate \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nTogether with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (1) this shows that the set of\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
