allel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.4 \u2013 Count the lines.*  \n   We have produced exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{vertical lines}) = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.5 \u2013 Verify the covering condition.*  \n   \u2013 If a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) belongs to a column \\(a\\in A\\), then by construction it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) that passes through the top point of that column; consequently it is covered.  \n   \u2013 If \\(a\\notin A\\), the whole column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) is contained in the vertical line \\(V_a\\); hence every point of that column is covered.  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion** \u2013 the constructions work for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Together with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) this proves that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nWith only \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most \\(n\\) of them can be sunny; therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Extremal constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n\n  \\]\n\n  are all non\u2011sunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so it lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Hence the covering condition holds.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For each column index \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,