3. Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines\n\nFor every column index \\(a\\notin A\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\; x = a .\n\\]\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\n\n#### 3.4. Number of lines\n\nWe have produced exactly  \n\n\\[\nk\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{vertical lines}) = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.\n\n#### 3.5. Verification that every required point is covered\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\in A\\):* the point lies in column \\(a\\).  By construction the sunny line\n\\(\\ell_i\\) with \\(a_i=a\\) passes through the top point of that column and has\nslope \\(i\\).  Because the line is straight, it contains **all** points of the\ncolumn whose \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(a\\); in particular \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\notin A\\):* the whole column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\) is\ncontained in the vertical line \\(V_a\\); thus \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed\nlines, satisfying the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all lines non\u2011sunny);\n* \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny);\n* every intermediate \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n  and \\(n-k\\) vertical lines).\n\nTogether with the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) from (1), this shows that the set\nof admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

