her slope.  \nEven if we choose them all to be vertical, each vertical line coincides with a\ncolumn and therefore covers **all** points of that column, i.e. at most\n\\(n\\) points (the longest column has length \\(n\\)).  \nThus the non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\nConsequently the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by the\nwhole family is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n|S_n\\cap\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L|\n   \\le kn + (n-k)n = n^{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut  \n\n\\[\n|S_n| = \\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 > n^{2}\n\\quad\\text{for every } n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore, when \\(k\\ge 1\\) it is impossible to cover all points of \\(S_n\\) with\nonly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nL_b:\\; y = b ,\\qquad b=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)),\nso \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Final conclusion\n\nThe covering condition can be satisfied with exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines **iff**\nno line is sunny.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

