nstead join **any two distinct points of \\(P_c\\) that are not the two extremes**.  Choose, for each \\(c\\) that we wish to replace, the points  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{(1)}=(2,c-2),\\qquad Q_c^{(2)}=(c-2,2),\n\\]\n\nwhich exist because \\(c\\ge 4\\) when we replace a line (the case \\(c=2,3\\) will be handled separately).  The slope of the line through \\(Q_c^{(1)}\\) and \\(Q_c^{(2)}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-(c-2)}{(c-2)-2}= \\frac{4-c}{c-4},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any integer \\(c\\ge 4\\).  Hence this line is sunny.  Denote it by \\(S_c\\).\n\nNow fix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\).  Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) we replace the first \\(t\\) lines \\(L_{c}\\) (with the smallest possible values of \\(c\\)) by the sunny lines \\(S_c\\) defined above, leaving the remaining \\(n-t\\) lines unchanged.  The resulting collection is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_t=\\bigl\\{\\,S_c\\mid c\\in C_t\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\\bigl\\{\\,L_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus C_t\\,\\bigr\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C_t\\) is any set of \\(t\\) indices chosen from \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) such that each chosen index is at least \\(4\\) (if \\(t\\) is larger than the number of indices \\(\\ge 4\\), we use the following ad\u2011hoc replacements for the remaining small indices: for \\(c=2\\) we replace \\(x+y=2\\) by the sunny line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,0)\\); for \\(c=3\\) we replace \\(x+y=3\\) by the sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); both replacements give slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nEach replacement line \\(S_c\\) still contains **all** points of \\(P_c\\) because the two points we used to define \\(S_c\\) belong to \\(P_c\\) and any other point of \\(P_c\\) lies on the same line \\(x+y=c\\), which coincides with the line through those two points (the three points are collinear).  Consequently every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) continues to lie on some line of \\(\\mathcal L_t\\).  \n\nThe lines in \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) are distinct: the original lines \\(L_c\\) are pairwise distinct, and each newly introduced sunny line \\(S_c\\) has a slope different from \\(-1\\), so it cannot coincide with any remaining line \\(L_{c'}\\).  Moreover the slopes of the different \\(S_c\\) are distinct because the pairs \\((Q_c^{(1)},Q_c^{(2)})\\) involve different coordinates, yielding different rational slopes.  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nBy construction exactly the \\(t\\) replaced lines are sunny, while the other \\(n-t\\) lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore not sunny.  Thus \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) contains precisely \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(t\\) was arbitrary, for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) there exists a configuration satisfying the required conditions and having exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_n .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n#### Conclusion  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(0\\in\\mathcal K_n\\); from Lemma\u202f2 we have \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_n\\).  Because a family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, it is impossible to have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn other words, **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines in a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]"
