+i,\\,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k-i+1 \\,\\},\n   \\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 3 – covering the corner with sunny lines.*  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{k}\\,x + \\bigl(1-\\frac{i-1}{k}\\bigr)(n-k+1) .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{k}\\).  Because \\(0\\le i-1\\le k-1\\) and \\(k\\ge 1\\),\n\n   * the slope is never \\(0\\) (it is \\(0\\) only when \\(i=1\\), but then the line is \\(y=1\\), which is **horizontal** and therefore forbidden; to avoid this we replace the case \\(i=1\\) by the line \\(x=n\\), which has infinite slope and is also forbidden – consequently we treat the two extreme indices separately, see below);\n   * the slope is never \\(\\infty\\) (no vertical line appears);\n   * the slope is never \\(-1\\) because \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{k}\\ge 0\\).\n\n   Hence every line \\(S_i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k\\) is sunny.\n\n   For the two remaining indices we use the following two sunny lines, which are not parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\):\n\n   \\[\n   S_1:\\; y = x-(n-2k+1),\\qquad\n   S_{k+1}:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{n+2}{2}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Both have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently they are sunny as well.\n\n   *Step 4 – verification that the sunny lines cover the corner.*  \n   Direct substitution shows that for each integer point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) there exists an index \\(i\\) (with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) such that \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\).  Indeed, solving (7) for \\(y\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{i-1}{k}\\,x + \\bigl(1-\\frac{i-1}{k}\\bigr)(n-k+1)\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\n   i = 1 + \\frac{k\\,(y-(n-k+1))}{x-(n-k+1)} .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   For a point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) the denominator \\(x-(n-k+1)\\) is a positive integer not exceeding \\(k-1\\), while the numerator \\(k\\,(y-(n-k+1))\\) is a non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(k(k-1)\\).  Consequently the right‑hand side of (9) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(k\\), i.e. some admissible index \\(i\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Step 5 – assembling the whole configuration.*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k := \\mathcal B_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of  \n\n   * \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines of the form \\(x+y=m\\) (the backbone);\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_i\\) constructed above.\n\n   All \\(n\\) lines are distinct: the backbone lines all have slope \\(-1\\), whereas each \\(S_i\\) has a slope different from \\(-1\\); moreover the slopes \\(\\frac{i-1}{k}\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le k\\) are pairwise distinct, and the two exceptional lines \\(S_1\\) and \\(S_{k+1}\\) have slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), respectively, which are also different from each other and from the backbone slope.\n\n   By construction the backbone covers every lattice point with \\(a+b\\le n-k+1\\); the sunny lines cover every lattice point of the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\).  Hence **every** lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the two requirements of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) together with the explicit construction for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) shows that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{D1}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound  \n\nA configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; therefore the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{U1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nr:=n-k .\n\\tag{C0}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The non‑sunny backbone  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_r:=\\{\\,L_m : x+y=m \\mid m=2,3,\\dots ,r+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{C1}\n\\]\n\nEach line \\(L_m\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\Delta_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le r+1\\) we have \\(a+b=m\\) for some \\(m\\in\\{2,\\dots ,r+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_m\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal B_r\\) covers all points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) whose sum does not exceed \\(r+1=n-k+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The uncovered corner  \n\nThe points of \\(\\Delta_n\\) that are **not** covered by \\(\\mathcal B_r\\) satisfy  \n\n\\[\na+b\\ge r+2=n-k+2 .\n\\tag{C2}\n\\]\n\nTogether with the constraints \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) this yields the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le k,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{C3}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right‑angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\); it contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\tag{C4}\n\\]\n\nlattice points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{k}\\,x + \\Bigl(1-\\frac{i-1}{k}\\Bigr)(n-k+1).\n\\tag{C5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{i-1}{k}\\).  Because \\(0\\le i-1\\le k-1\\),\n\n* the slope is never \\(-1\\);\n* it is never \\(0\\) except when \\(i=1\\); the case \\(i=1\\) is handled separately (see below);\n* it is never infinite.\n\nHence every line \\(S_i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k\\) is sunny.\n\nFor the remaining index we use the line  \n\n\\[\nS_1:\\; y = x-(n-2k+1),\n\\tag{C6}\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(1\\) and is therefore sunny as well.\n\nNow we prove that the family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\).  Because \\(x\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(x\\le n\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\nd:=x-(n-k+1)\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(0\\le d\\le k-1\\).  Moreover \\(y\\le n+1-x\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\ny-(n-k+1)\\le k-d .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=1+\\frac{k\\bigl(y-(n-k+1)\\bigr)}{d}\\qquad\\text{if }d>0,\n\\tag{C7}\n\\]\n\nand set \\(i:=1\\) when \\(d=0\\) (which corresponds to the vertical side of the triangle).  \nBecause \\(0\\le y-(n-k+1)\\le k-d\\), the fraction in (C7) is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(k-1\\); consequently \\(i\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\n\nA straightforward computation shows that the point \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_i\\).  Indeed, substituting \\(i\\) from (C7) into (C5) yields exactly the relation defining \\(i\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.4  The final configuration  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\mathcal B_{\\,n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{S_1,S_2,\\dots ,S_k\\}.\n\\tag{C8}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (all of slope \\(-1\\)) and \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)’s).  All lines are distinct because the slopes of the \\(S_i\\)’s are different from \\(-1\\) and pairwise distinct among themselves.\n\nBy the discussion in §§2.1–2.3, the backbone \\(\\mathcal B_{n-k}\\) covers every point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) with sum \\(\\le n-k+1\\), while the sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\}\\) cover the remaining points of the triangle \\(T_k\\).  Hence **every** point of \\(\\Delta_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines (upper bound (U1));\n* For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed above fulfills the covering condition and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
