ctions: the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, and the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Hence among the lines listed above only the two extreme lines \\(x=1\\) (vertical) and \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) are *not* sunny; all the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines \\(x+y=t\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore **not** sunny either.  \n   Thus the *default* configuration contains **zero** sunny lines.\n\n4. **Creating sunny lines.**  \n   We may replace any of the lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) by a different line that still passes through the same diagonal \\(D_{2}\\) or \\(D_{n+1}\\) but is not parallel to the forbidden directions.  \n   For the diagonal \\(D_{2}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) we can choose any line through \\((1,1)\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); similarly for \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \n   Each such replacement increases the number of sunny lines by one, and the replacements are independent because the two diagonals are disjoint.  \n\n   Consequently we can obtain configurations with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k=0,\\;k=1,\\;k=2\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines by replacing none, one, or both of the extreme lines.\n\n5. **Maximum number of sunny lines.**  \n   To increase the number of sunny lines beyond two we must also modify some of the lines \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(3\\le t\\le n\\)).  \n   Replacing a line \\(x+y=t\\) by any other line that still contains the diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) forces the new line to pass through the two extreme points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\).  \n   The only line through these two points is precisely \\(x+y=t\\); any other line through the same diagonal would miss at least one of its points, contradicting the covering condition.  \n   Hence the lines \\(x+y=t\\) cannot be altered, and they remain non‑sunny.  \n\n   Therefore the **largest** possible number of sunny lines is obtained by making both extreme lines sunny, i.e. \\(k=n-2\\).  \n\n6. **Symmetry argument for the remaining values.**  \n   By interchanging the roles of the \\(x