quently each value of \\(a+b\\) can be attained at most once on \\(\\ell\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nEvery admissible family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition must contain **exactly one** line that meets each diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) (\\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy the covering condition each diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) is contained in the union of the lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nIf a line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) met two different diagonals, Lemma 1 would force it to meet each of those diagonals in exactly one point, leaving at least one point of each of those diagonals uncovered – a contradiction.  \nThus each line can intersect at most one diagonal, and because there are \\(n\\) diagonals and \\(n\\) lines, each diagonal must be intersected by a distinct line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3  \nFor \\(3\\le t\\le n\\) the unique line that contains the diagonal \\(D_{t}\\) is the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}:\\;x+y=t .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe extreme points of \\(D_{t}\\) are \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\).  \nThe line through these two points has equation \\(x+y=t\\).  \nAny other line through one of the points would have a different slope and would miss the other extreme point, hence could not contain the whole diagonal. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4  \nThe diagonals \\(D_{2}\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) consist of the single points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) respectively; any line passing through such a point satisfies the covering condition for that diagonal.\n\n*Proof.* Immediate from the definition of the diagonals. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemmas 2–4 we obtain the **canonical configuration**\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=t\\mid 3\\le t\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIt consists