** sunny.  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructing configurations with sunny lines  \n\nThe two lines that meet the singleton diagonals \\(D_{2}\\) and \\(D_{n+1}\\) are completely free (Lemma 4).  \nWe may replace them by any lines that pass through the same points, provided the replacements are distinct from each other and from the forced lines.\n\n* If we choose both replacements to be parallel to a forbidden direction (horizontal, vertical, or slope \\(-1\\)), we obtain again a configuration with **0** sunny lines.\n* If exactly one of the two replacements has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), we obtain a configuration with **1** sunny line.\n* If both replacements have slopes different from the three forbidden values, we obtain a configuration with **2** sunny lines.\n\nThus the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are all attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nThe \\(n-2\\) lines \\(x+y=t\\) (\\(3\\le t\\le n\\)) are forced by Lemma 3; each has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore never sunny.  \nConsequently any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, so the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(2\\).\n\nTo obtain \\(k\\ge 3\\) we would need at least one of the forced lines to be sunny, which is impossible because its slope is uniquely \\(-1\\).  \nHence no integer \\(k\\) with \\(k\\ge 3\\) can be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion  \n\nThe only non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
