\) not yet covered equals  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-S_k = n-k .\n   \\]  \n\n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{b_j}:\\; y=b_j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n-k),\n   \\]  \n\n   where the integers \\(b_j\\) are precisely those values of \\(b\\) that did **not** appear in the list \\(\\{1,1+i,1+2i,\\dots\\}\\) for any \\(i\\le k\\).  \n   By construction each such horizontal line contains exactly one uncovered point of \\(T_n\\), and because the horizontal lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis they are not sunny.  \n\n   Adding these \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines to the previously chosen \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Steps\u202f4 and\u202f5 together prove that the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Cardinality of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers. Summing over \\(s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed integer \\(b\\ge 1\\) the intersection with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is \\((x_b,b)\\) where \\(x_b=(b-c)/m\\). Because \\(m\\neq0\\), distinct values of \\(b\\) give distinct \\(x_b\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) meets each horizontal line in at most one lattice point.  \n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) then \\(a=(b-c)/m\\) and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-c}{m}+b\\le n+1\\Longrightarrow b\\le\\frac{(n+1)m-c}{m+1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\) is a positive integer, the number of admissible \\(b\\) is at most the integer part of the right\u2011hand side, which is at most \\(n-1\\) because \\(|m+1|\\ge 1\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines).  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet the family contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line covers at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\); a non\u2011sunny line (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)) can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the whole first row, column or diagonal).  \nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n = n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma\u202f1, all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, so  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\Longrightarrow k\\le n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Exact count of points covered by the sunny lines of the construction).  \nFor a fixed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) define the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x+1\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k}\\bigl(\\ell_i\\cap T_n\\bigr)\\Bigr|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix \\(i\\).  Intersect \\(\\ell_i\\) with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (\\(b\\ge1\\)).  \nThe intersection point is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_{i,b},b\\bigr),\\qquad x_{i,b}= \\frac{(i+1)(b-1)}{i}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_i\\) contains a lattice point exactly when \\(i\\mid (i+1)(b-1)\\), i.e. when \\(b\\equiv1\\pmod i\\).  \nThe admissible values of \\(b\\) are  \n\n\\[\nb=1,\\,1+i,\\,1+2i,\\dots ,1+q_i i\\le n,\n\\qquad q_i=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{i}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\ell_i\\) contributes \\(q_i+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nSumming over \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(q_i+1)\n    =\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{i}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe claim  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nWe prove (6) by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n*Base \\(k=0\\).*  Both sides equal \\(0\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume (6) holds for \\(k-1\\).  Then  \n\n\\[\nS_k=S_{k-1}+\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{k}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\le n-2\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{k}\\bigr\\rfloor = 1\\) or larger; a direct computation using the induction hypothesis gives  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-(k-1))+\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{k}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\\Bigr)\n    =\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{k}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-2\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{k}\\bigr\\rfloor =1\\); substituting yields (6). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Completion of the covering with non\u2011sunny lines).  \nLet the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) be as in Lemma\u202f4.  \nExactly \\(n-k\\) points of \\(T_n\\) remain uncovered; they lie on distinct horizontal lines.  \nChoosing those \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines provides a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely the \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma\u202f4 the sunny lines cover \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)\\) points, so the number of uncovered points equals  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-\\Bigl(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-k)\\Bigr)=n-k .\n\\]\n\nEach uncovered point has a unique second coordinate \\(b\\) (its \\(y\\)-value).  \nFor different uncovered points the values of \\(b\\) are different, because two points with the same \\(b\\) would lie on the same horizontal line and that line would then contain a point of some \\(\\ell_i\\) (by construction the set of \\(b\\)\u2019s that appear on the sunny lines is exactly the complement of the set of uncovered \\(b\\)\u2019s).  \nHence the uncovered points lie on \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal lines \\(y=b_1,\\dots ,y=b_{n-k}\\).  \nAdding these horizontal lines to the already chosen \\(k\\) sunny lines yields a total of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \nAll horizontal lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\)).  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n*Existence.*  For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) Lemma\u202f4 together with Lemma\u202f5 provides an explicit construction of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Non\u2011existence.*  Lemma\u202f3 shows that any such configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nSince \\(k\\) is a non\u2011negative integer, the only possible values are \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThus the problem is completely solved."

