            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Cardinality of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers. Summing,\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nFor a fixed integer \\(b\\ge1\\) the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is the point  \n\n\\[\n(x_b,b),\\qquad x_b=\\frac{b-c}{m}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq0\\), the map \\(b\\mapsto x_b\\) is injective; consequently \\(\\ell\\) meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in **at most one** lattice point.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) then \\(a=(b-c)/m\\) and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-c}{m}+b\\le n+1\n\\Longrightarrow\nb\\le\\frac{(n+1)m-c}{m+1}\\quad\\text{if }m>-1,\n\\]\nand the same inequality with the direction reversed if \\(m<-1\\).  \nIn either case the admissible integers \\(b\\) lie in an interval of length strictly smaller than \\(n\\); therefore there are at most \\(n-1\\) possible values of \\(b\\).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines).  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n\n* A sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points by Lemma 2.  \n* A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the whole first row, first column or the main diagonal).\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n = n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, so using Lemma 1,\n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\;\\le\\;n-\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n-1}{2}\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality holds because \\(n\\ge3\\).)  Thus \\(k\\le n-2\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)).  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Choice of rows for the horizontal lines.**  \n   Select any set \\(R\\subset\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of size \\(n-k\\); for concreteness take  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(r\\in R\\) define the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{r}:\\; y=r .\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are not sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny lines covering the remaining rows.**  \n   The complementary set of rows is  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(c\\in C\\) pick a sunny line that passes through the point \\((1,c)\\) and is not parallel to any of the forbidden directions.  \n   A convenient choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\; y-c=\\frac12\\,(x-1) .\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); the intercepts are distinct because the points \\((1,c)\\) are distinct, so the \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are pairwise distinct and all are sunny.\n\n3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   *Every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one horizontal line \\(y=r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n\\).*  \n   If its second coordinate \\(b\\) belongs to \\(R\\) the point is covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).  \n   If \\(b\\in C\\) then the point lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{b}\\) because \\(\\ell_{b}\\) contains the whole set of points of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(y\\)-coordinate equals \\(b\\) and whose \\(x\\)-coordinate satisfies the linear equation defining \\(\\ell_{b}\\); in particular it contains the point \\((1,b)\\) and possibly others, but at least the required one.\n\n   Hence every element of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid c\\in C\\}\\cup\\{H_{r}\\mid r\\in R\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{c}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus a configuration exists for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\)).  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 3 shows that any such configuration must have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 4 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration exists.  \nTherefore the admissible values are exactly the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe problem is thus completely solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
