\), the bound (1) is sharp.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 shows that any admissible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 4 provides, for each \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit configuration meeting all requirements.  \n   Hence the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Cardinality of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions in positive integers. Summing,\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line).  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a line whose slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\). Then  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nFor an integer ordinate \\(b\\ge1\\) the intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) is the point  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(x_b,b\\bigr),\\qquad x_b=\\frac{b-c}{m}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(m\\neq0\\), the map \\(b\\mapsto x_b\\) is injective; consequently \\(\\ell\\) meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in **at most one** lattice point.\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap T_n\\) then \\(a=(b-c)/m\\) and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-c}{m}+b\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInequality (2) is linear in \\(b\\). Solving for \\(b\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb\\le \\frac{(n+1)m-c}{m+1}\\quad\\text{when }m>-1,\n\\qquad\nb\\ge \\frac{(n+1)m-c}{m+1}\\quad\\text{when }m<-1 .\n\\]\n\nIn either case the admissible integers \\(b\\) lie in an interval whose length is strictly smaller than \\(n\\); indeed the two endpoints differ by  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{|m+1|}<n ,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(|m+1|>1\\) (the excluded slopes are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\)).  \nHence at most \\(n-1\\) distinct ordinates can occur, and therefore \\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Upper bound for the number of sunny lines).  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\), the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n* By Lemma 2 a sunny line contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n* A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the longest possible row, column or diagonal).\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\,(n-1)+(n-k)\\,n=n^{2}-k .\n\\]\n\nUsing Lemma 1, all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, so  \n\n\\[\nn^{2}-k\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le n^{2}-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=n-2 .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Realisation for every admissible \\(k\\)).  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Selection of rows.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   C=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad R=\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\in C\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{c}:\\; y-c=\\frac12\\,(x-1)\n   \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{equivalently }y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12-c\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); thus each \\(\\ell_{c}\\) is sunny.  \n   Distinct values of \\(c\\) give distinct lines because they pass through the distinct points \\((1,c)\\).  \n   Each \\(\\ell_{c}\\) contains the point \\((1,c)\\in T_n\\).\n\n3. **Horizontal lines.**  \n   For every \\(r\\in R\\) draw the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{r}:\\; y=r .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are distinct because the ordinates are distinct.\n\n4. **Coverage.**  \n   Take an arbitrary point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\in R\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\in C\\)*, then \\(b\\le k\\).  Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_{b}\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   b-b=\\frac12\\,(a-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the only point of \\(T_n\\) with ordinate \\(b\\) that belongs to \\(\\ell_{b}\\) is \\((1,b)\\), and this point is indeed covered by \\(\\ell_{b}\\).  \n   All other points with ordinate \\(b\\) have abscissa \\(a\\ge2\\); for such points we have \\(b\\ge k+1\\) (otherwise they would violate the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  Consequently they belong to the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\) with \\(b\\in R\\).\n\n   Thus every element of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one line of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_{c}\\mid c\\in C\\}\\cup\\{H_{r}\\mid r\\in R\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and it satisfies the two conditions of the problem. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Complete description of admissible \\(k\\)).  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 3 gives the universal upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma 4 shows that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a suitable configuration exists.  \nTherefore the admissible values are precisely those listed. ∎"
